#pragma once

// UniverseDensityCore.ush
// Cosmic-web density field. TAKES RESOURCES: the domain warp is a Texture3D fetch,
// so this file is compiled by C++ behind GalaxyHLSLShim.h exactly as the galaxy core
// is -- the shim stubs Texture3D::SampleLevel to a neutral 0.5, and the C++ field
// therefore reduces to the UNWARPED analytic web while the shader gets the warped
// one. That divergence is deliberate and bounded: it is a displacement of at most
// WarpAmount cells, it affects nothing that is currently consuming the CPU field,
// and entity generation for this layer lands on the compute harness the galaxy layer
// already uses, which samples the same texture the shader does. UniverseDensity.ush
// is now the ray march and nothing else -- warp, regional variance, the analytic
// web, and the density composition all live here, in SampleAtPosition.
//
// PARITY RULES -- both compilers see this file:
//   - Suffix every float literal (0.5f, not 0.5). C++ types an unsuffixed literal as
//     double, which promotes whole expressions and rounds differently from the GPU.
//   - Guard every pow() base away from zero.
//   - No swizzles beyond .x/.y/.z/.w, no integer vectors, no HLSL-only intrinsics
//     outside the shim.
//
// STRUCTURE: the web is the SKELETON of a cellular decomposition. Space is divided
// into cells, each holding one hashed node, and each node is the centre of a VOID.
// The features are the elements of the skeleton between the nodes, identified by how
// many nodes are simultaneously nearest:
//
//   1 node closest        void       -- the interior of a cell
//   2 nodes equidistant   wall       -- a face, the sheet between two voids
//   3 nodes equidistant   filament   -- an edge, where three sheets meet
//   4 nodes equidistant   cluster    -- a vertex, where four filaments converge
//
// NOTHING IS RANKED ANY MORE. "How many nodes are simultaneously nearest" used to be
// answered by sorting the neighbourhood into F1/F2/F3 and reading the differences:
// F2-F1 small meant two nodes tied, F3-F1 small meant three did. It is now answered
// directly, and continuously, by an EFFECTIVE PARTICIPANT COUNT. Give every candidate
// a weight that falls off with distance, normalize the weights to sum to one, and
// take the inverse participation ratio:
//
//     p_i = w_i / sum_j w_j        N = 1 / sum_i p_i^2 = (sum w)^2 / (sum w^2)
//
// N is 1 where one node dominates, 2 where two tie, 3 where three do, 4 at a vertex.
// It is exactly what the rank differences were measuring, without the sort. Two
// running sums replace a three-deep insertion chain, both sums are reassociable so the
// unrolled 27 has no serial dependency left at all, and the value is smooth in
// position rather than merely continuous.
//
// WHY THIS AND NOT THE SORT. Sorted order statistics of a FIXED set are already
// continuous -- min and max of continuous functions are continuous, and a rank swap
// inside the window costs a gradient kink, not a jump. The jumps came from the SET
// changing: crossing a cell plane retires one 3x3x3 slab and admits another, and a
// departing node that was inside the top three steps F2 or F3 discontinuously. Soft
// weights fix that to exactly the degree that a candidate at the window edge carries
// negligible weight, which is a checkable condition rather than a hope -- see the
// truncation bound at WeightedNodeMeasure, which is where the remaining exposure
// lives and is stated in numbers.
//
// CLUSTERS ARE STILL NOT ANALYTIC, but they could be again for free. A fourth rank,
// F4, used to be tracked so F4-F1 could measure cluster-ness; that came out, and the
// noise-mask stage that replaced it has since come out too. The participation count
// hands the geometric version back at no cost -- the vertex case is just N ~ 4, one
// more saturate off the same two sums -- so whatever replaces the mask has that
// available for free, and it is one line in DensityFromWeb rather than a stage.
//
// ORGANICS: an unweighted Euclidean diagram has planar faces, straight edges and one
// void size, which is most of why a cellular field reads as manufactured. Three
// corrections, none of which cost a hash in the 27-cell loop:
//   - a per-node power-diagram offset, so voids differ in size
//   - two fetches from the packed noise volume supplying a two-octave domain warp
//   - one fetch from the packed noise volume supplying the regional variance
//
// THE PER-NODE ANISOTROPIC METRIC IS GONE. It measured distance in each node's own
// stretched space so bisectors came out as quadrics rather than planes, and the warp
// now does that job better: it curves the bisectors without touching the metric, so
// it costs nothing in the ranking loop and nothing in the search guarantee. What the
// metric cost was 22% of the ranking loop, an inflation of every in-span distance
// that pushed true neighbours out of the searched window, and a stretch magnitude of
// |s|^2 that was largest for nodes near a cell CORNER -- a modulation locked to the
// cell grid, in a field whose visible failure was cell-grid-aligned.
//
// COORDINATES: sampled as an integer cell plus a fractional offset, not a single
// float3. That is not premature -- virtual traversal reaches magnitudes near 1e11,
// where float32 has no fractional precision left and a cell-relative formulation is
// the only thing that survives. SampleAtPosition does the decomposition for now;
// when traversal lands, the caller supplies cell and frac directly and that helper
// goes away rather than being unpicked.
//
// It now decomposes TWICE, once per lattice, and the second split is where the
// formulation earns itself: re-expressing a 1e11 cell count in a coarser lattice's
// units is exact under integer division and catastrophically inexact as a float
// multiply. See TWO LATTICES, ONE BLEND.

// ==========================================================================
// COST PROBE -- temporary, for bisecting an instruction count
// ==========================================================================
// A ladder that removes one stage per level AT COMPILE TIME. Read the material's
// instruction count at each level; the difference between two rungs is what that
// stage actually costs. Leave it at 0 in anything shipped.
//
//   0  everything
//   1  no regional variance
//   2  also no domain warp
//   3  also no neighbourhood search -- one cell per lattice instead of twenty-seven
//
// RENUMBERED when the cluster/gas stage came out; level 1 used to remove its detail
// fetch and there is no such fetch any more. A new stage that adds one should take
// level 1 back and push the rest down, since the outermost thing is the cheapest to
// bisect away first.
//
// WHY A COMPILE-TIME LADDER AND NOT THE PARAMETERS. bWarpEnable, bRegionEnableA,
// bRegionEnableB, bLatticeLarge and bShape are RUNTIME branches, so both sides of each are
// compiled and both are counted. Setting WarpAmount to zero in a material instance
// removes the work at runtime and changes the reported instruction count by nothing
// at all. The number describes the union of every feature path in the file, whatever
// the pins are set to, which is why it can only be bisected with defines.
//
// Expect the last step to be the largest single drop by a wide margin. The
// neighbourhood search is a 3x3x3 loop with compile-time bounds, so it is FULLY
// UNROLLED: twenty-seven copies of the hash, the squared distance and the two weight
// exponentials, on the order of a thousand instructions and the floor under everything
// else here.
//
// Note that level 4 does not merely make the field meaningless, it makes it EMPTY: a
// single candidate gives (sum w)^2 / (sum w^2) = 1 identically, so both feature
// measures read as pure void and the density collapses to the floor. That is fine for
// an instruction count and useless for anything else.
#ifndef UNIVERSE_COST_PROBE
#define UNIVERSE_COST_PROBE 0
#endif

#define UNIVERSE_PI 3.14159265358979323846f
#define UNIVERSE_POW_EPSILON 1e-6f

// ==========================================================================
// THE NODE WEIGHT -- THREE CONSTANTS
// ==========================================================================
// The weight is exp2(lambda * (pivot - u)), where u is the power-diagram squared
// distance to a candidate node. These three fix how lambda comes off an authored
// width and how the exponent is kept inside float32. See WeightedNodeMeasure for what
// the weight is FOR.
//
// WIDTH_TO_LAMBDA is a CALIBRATION, chosen so an authored width means the same
// thickness it meant under the ranked formulation and the material's existing values
// carry over rather than needing a retune. Derivation, then the measurement that
// confirms it:
//
//   The old wall term was (1 - x^2)^2 on x = (F2-F1)/width, and F2-F1 is 2*offset from
//   the wall surface, so it fell to half density at 0.27 * width from the surface.
//   The measure falls to half when the competing node's weight ratio r satisfies
//   (1+r)^2/(1+r^2) = 3/2, which is r = 2 - sqrt(3) = 0.268, so lambda*du = 1.90. Two
//   nodes at distance d either side of a wall differ by du = 4*d*x, and d is about
//   0.55 for one node per cell, so the half-density offset is 0.86/lambda. Equating
//   the two gives lambda = 0.86 / (0.27 * width) = 3.2 / width.
//
//   Measured rather than trusted: matching the volume fraction above half density,
//   wall term only, 130x130x60 samples, the best lambda came out at 3.24/width for an
//   authored 0.08, 3.21/width for 0.06 and 2.83/width for 0.04. Stable across the
//   range, and the 3.2 is the analytic number rather than a fit to it.
//
// WEIGHT_PIVOT recentres the exponent and does nothing else -- the measure is invariant
// under a common scaling of every weight, so subtracting a constant from every u is
// free. It exists because the exponent is doubled by the squared accumulator, which
// halves the exponent range float32 can carry. 0.31 is the MEAN squared distance from
// a sample to its nearest node, measured over 400k samples (max 1.73, and 99.7% of
// samples below 1.0), so the dominant weight sits near 1 and the sum near unit
// magnitude for a typical sample. The spread term tracks the pivot down as the
// power-diagram offset pulls u negative, which is what keeps the TOP of the range
// bounded no matter how large the offset is authored.
//
// LAMBDA_MAX is the one clamp in this file that is not a division guard. With the
// pivot above, the squared accumulator's exponent spans about
// +-2*lambda*(0.31 + 0.5*spread), and float32 overflows past 2^127. 100 keeps that
// inside the range for any spread below about 0.65 -- comfortably wider than the
// truncation bound at WeightedNodeMeasure allows anyway -- and corresponds to a
// feature width of 0.032 cells, a wall three percent of a void across.
//
// The failure past it is benign and worth knowing: what underflows first is the
// dominant weight on samples whose nearest node is unusually FAR, which is 0.3% of
// samples and all of them deep in void. Both sums flush to zero, the division guard
// returns zero, and the saturates read that as void -- which is the right answer for
// those samples for independent reasons.
//
// EXP_HEADROOM bounds the weight exponent from ABOVE, directly, one min per candidate.
// It is what makes overflow impossible for every parameter combination rather than
// merely unlikely for sensible ones, and overflow here produces a NaN that the march
// spreads down the whole ray. 55 caps a single weight at 2^55 and the squared
// accumulator's fifty-four-term sum at 2^116.
//
// It used to do this job by lowering the pivot instead, which is a shift and therefore
// free. That does not survive two lattices: the lowest u a candidate can reach is
// -spread * ratio^2, and at ratio 3 a pivot low enough to cover it sits so far below the
// typical u that every weight in the field underflows to zero and the layer returns pure
// void. The pivot has to stay where the dominant candidate is, so the bound moved onto
// the exponent. See LatticeWeightSums.
#define UNIVERSE_WIDTH_TO_LAMBDA 3.2f
#define UNIVERSE_WEIGHT_PIVOT 0.31f
#define UNIVERSE_LAMBDA_MAX 100.0f
#define UNIVERSE_EXP_HEADROOM 55.0f

// ==========================================================================
// TWO LATTICES, ONE DIAGRAM
// ==========================================================================
// CellSize is a packed (small, large, bias, free) now, and the layer walks TWO
// neighbourhoods -- one fine, one coarse -- and feeds BOTH into a single pair of
// weight accumulators. There is one tessellation, not two. This is the only sound way
// to make void size vary spatially: a single lattice whose cell size varies with
// position is not a lattice at all, because neighbouring samples disagree about which
// cell they are in, the int/frac split stops being a partition, and the field tears
// rather than bends. Two fixed lattices each keep their own exact partition, and the
// diagram they jointly define is continuous everywhere.
//
// A CROSSFADE OF TWO FINISHED DENSITIES IS NOT THIS, and was the first thing tried.
// lerp(D_fine, D_coarse, t) puts two complete webs on top of each other at partial
// brightness through every transition band -- a varying overlap, with fine walls
// passing through coarse voids and neither owning the space. What is wanted is
// STRUCTURAL variance: one web whose cells are small here and large there, with big
// voids abutting small ones and sharing walls, the way a real cellular structure with
// mixed cell sizes looks. That needs the two node sets competing inside one diagram,
// which is what this does.
//
// TWO THINGS ARE REQUIRED TO MAKE ONE DIAGRAM OUT OF TWO LATTICES, and each fixes a
// failure the other does not.
//
//   A COMMON METRIC. Every distance is converted to SMALL-CELL units before it becomes
//   a weight -- the coarse lattice's squared distances are multiplied by the squared
//   ratio, and its spread with them. Without this each lattice's nearest node has a
//   weight near 1 by construction, so every sample has one strong participant from each
//   lattice, N reads 2 everywhere, and saturate(N - 1) pins the wall term at full
//   across the entire field. Solid white, not a web. In a common metric only one node
//   is genuinely nearest, and a near-tie between any two candidates -- fine, coarse, or
//   one of each -- is a real bisector, because they are competing for the same
//   territory in the same units.
//
//   A SUPPRESSION, ONE-SIDED. In a common metric a fine lattice always has a nearer
//   node than a coarse one, so on its own the coarse lattice would never appear at all.
//   The region field therefore adds a uniform amount to every FINE candidate's power
//   distance, shrinking every fine node's territory together until the coarse nodes
//   claim what is left. The coarse lattice is never scaled: it does not need to be,
//   because it wins by default once the fine one steps back. That asymmetry is not a
//   simplification, it is the shape of the problem.
//
// Removing a node by shrinking its territory is exactly the mechanism VoidSizeSkew
// already uses on individual nodes, applied to a whole lattice at once -- which is why
// it is continuous where a hash threshold that deleted nodes outright would pop.
//
// WHERE THE CROSSOVER SITS. The mean power distance to a sample's nearest node is
// UNIVERSE_WEIGHT_PIVOT in that lattice's own units, so in shared units the fine
// lattice sits at that and the coarse at ratio^2 times it. The gap between them is
// therefore pivot * (ratio^2 - 1), and the suppression runs to TWICE that across the
// blend -- which puts the crossover at the middle of the range rather than jammed
// against one end. CellSizeBias reshapes where within the range a region lands.
//
// The transition is not a knife edge at that midpoint, and this is the whole point:
// the gap between a given sample's own fine and coarse distances varies by far more
// than the width of a wall, so at any blend near the crossover some places have a fine
// node winning and others a coarse one. That mixture IS the mixed-scale structure.
//
// THE COST IS THE HONEST PART. This doubles the neighbourhood walk, which is the
// dominant cost in the file -- 54 hashes and 54 exponentials where there were 27. Two
// things claw most of it back. The lattices collapse to one when the two cell sizes
// round to the same ratio, which is the single-lattice configuration and costs nothing
// extra. And each walk is skipped per sample once its own candidates cannot matter,
// which is the saving that decides whether this feature costs one walk or two.
//
// THE THRESHOLD IS AN EXPONENT, NOT A BLEND POSITION. Testing the blend against a small
// epsilon on t skipped the fine walk only above t = 0.998, so it caught the exact
// endpoint and nothing else. What decides whether the fine lattice matters is the largest
// weight it can produce, and the suppression puts a hard floor under its power distance:
// u is at least suppress - spread whatever the hash returns, so the ceiling on its
// exponent is lambda * (pivot + spread - suppress). Once that is UNIVERSE_LATTICE_SKIP
// powers of two below the coarse lattice's dominant candidate, which the pivot holds near
// zero, the fine walk is pure cost. At ratio 3 with a 0.04 width that skips it above
// about t = 0.75.
//
// 40 is generous, and this one is a genuine bound rather than an average, so the margin
// is insurance against the pivot drifting from where the coarse dominant actually sits
// rather than against the spread of a distribution.
//
// ONLY THE FINE WALK IS SKIPPABLE. See the note at the skip itself for why the coarse one
// is not, and for the measurement that established it.
//
// Neither saving shows up in a material's reported instruction count, which counts the
// union of every path. See the COST PROBE note above for why that number cannot be read
// as cost here.
#define UNIVERSE_LATTICE_SKIP 40.0f

// ==========================================================================
// THE STEP FLOOR THE FIELD ITSELF IMPLIES
// ==========================================================================
// The march sizes its step by camera distance, so near the viewer the step becomes
// arbitrarily small and many successive samples land inside one cell -- recomputing the
// same fifty-four hashes for a field that has not changed between them. The authored
// MinStep pin exists to floor that, but it is an absolute distance in normalized units
// and has no idea what the cell size is, so it cannot be right for two cell sizes at
// once and goes stale the moment CellSizeRange moves.
//
// The field's own resolution is what should set the floor. The finest thing it can
// express is a wall, whose thickness is the authored feature width measured in SMALL
// cells -- the coarse tier's walls are `ratio` times thicker in world terms, since its
// lambda is divided by the ratio, so the fine tier is what binds. In normalized units
// that is width / InvCellSizeSmall, and half of it is the step below which extra samples
// are resolving detail the field does not contain.
//
// HALF, not a quarter or a tenth, because the accumulation is exact for constant density
// over a step -- the exponential is integrated analytically, not approximated -- so
// oversampling a smooth stretch buys nothing at all. What it protects is the wall
// crossing itself, and half a wall thickness puts at least two samples inside every wall.
//
// THE MINIMUM OF THE RANGE, since width is on the variance path and a region can resolve
// anywhere between the two authored ends. A floor built from the wider end would step
// over walls wherever the field resolves to the narrower one.
//
// THIS ONLY EVER RAISES THE FLOOR. It is combined with the authored pin by taking the
// larger, so an author who wants coarser steps still gets them and one who wants finer
// than the field can express no longer silently pays for them. MinSamples still overrides
// both, since the march clamps against maxStep afterwards -- coverage of the chord is
// still guaranteed however large this makes the step.
#define UNIVERSE_MIN_STEP_FRACTION 0.5f

// The skew exponent is applied to every one of the 27 candidates, so a pow() here is
// 27 transcendental pairs per march step -- log2 and exp2 each at a quarter rate.
// Defining this to a small whole number replaces them with repeated squaring, and
// costs the ability to modulate the skew regionally or animate it: set
// UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED to 0 to get the general pow path back.
//
// 4 is the shipped default and is exactly two multiplies.
#define UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED 4

// One 32-bit avalanche split into four 8-bit lanes, instead of four chained LCGs
// each given three rounds of xor-shift-multiply. Measured at 12% of the ranking
// loop; the four-value version was producing 32 bits of quality per channel to place
// a node whose position only has to be good to a fraction of a filament's width. A
// node on a 1/256 grid is displaced by 0.4% of a cell, which is 3% of a filament.
//
// This CHANGES THE FIELD -- same statistics, different universe -- so it is not a
// drop-in for a world already built against the old hash. Set to 0 to get it back.
#define UNIVERSE_FAST_HASH 1

// ==========================================================================
// THE DOMAIN WARP -- TWO OCTAVES, ONE TEXTURE
// ==========================================================================
// The hash value lattice is gone. It could not be incommensurate with the cell grid
// -- its index came from shifting the cell index, so its period was structurally
// forced to a power-of-two multiple of the cell size and its own lattice planes
// landed on cell planes forever. A texture's frequency is a float and its content is
// unrelated to anything, which is what stopped the cell boundaries reading as a
// contiguous disparity.
//
// TWO OCTAVES, SUMMED, BOTH SAMPLED AT THE UNWARPED COORDINATE. The large one warps
// the overarching structure of the whole lattice; the small one breaks up the
// geometry and gives the gas-like read. Sampling both at the same unwarped cell and
// fraction rather than chaining them -- large first, small evaluated at the warped
// result -- is deliberate: chaining makes the second fetch DEPENDENT on the first and
// the texture unit stalls waiting for it, and at a ten-to-one scale ratio the two
// forms are hard to tell apart. Both address computations are done first so both
// fetches issue together.
//
// THE OCTAVES SHARE ONE FOLD BUDGET. Displacement is a sum, so its Jacobian is the
// sum of the two Jacobians and total shear is
//
//     (AmountLarge * ScaleLarge + AmountSmall * ScaleSmall) * G
//
// Past one the map folds -- neighbouring samples cross over and the web tears rather
// than bends. Raising either octave eats the other's headroom, which is not obvious
// from the pins and is the thing most likely to be got wrong.
//
// WHAT G IS AND WHY IT IS A PIN. It is the largest gradient the filtered texture
// reaches per unit UV -- a property of the ASSET, not of this file, which is why it
// stopped being a #define the moment the plan called for a library of interchangeable
// warp volumes. It feeds the fold ceiling above and the march's empty-space bound
// below, and it is wrong in both directions: understate it and the march skips
// further than it may and steps over features while the mean error stays negligible;
// overstate it and the skip collapses and the march does work it did not need to.
//
// A 64^3 three-octave smooth volume measures 14.09. If a set of amplitudes looks
// right in the editor but the predicted shear is far above one, that is the
// measurement disagreeing with the pin, and the ratio between where the web ACTUALLY
// tears and 1/(Scale * G) is exactly the factor to correct it by.
//
// THIS PIN SHOULD EVENTUALLY BE 1 AND THEN DISAPPEAR. The right place to handle a
// per-asset gradient is the bake, not the shader: rescale each volume so its max
// gradient is exactly one per unit UV and G drops out of every expression here, shear
// becomes Amount * Scale directly, the fold ceiling becomes 1/Scale for every asset
// in the library, and -- the part that matters for a grab bag -- amplitudes tuned
// against one volume still mean the same thing against the next. Until that utility
// exists this pin is the bridge.

// Precision wrap, in cells, as a power of two. The UV is built as
//     (cell & MASK + frac) * WarpScale
// so the integer part is MASKED, never converted -- exact at 1e11, where a UV derived
// from a reconstructed float3 world position has no fractional bits left at all. This
// is the whole reason a texture was previously ruled out at this layer, and masking
// the cell index is what removes the objection.
//
// 4096 cells. The wrap is seamless rather than merely distant: MakeUniverseDensityParams
// QUANTIZES both scales so that WRAP * scale is a whole number, which makes the UV on
// either side of the wrap congruent modulo one and therefore identical under the
// sampler's repeat addressing -- value and every derivative. The quantum is 1/4096 of
// a repeat per cell, far below any tuning granularity that matters.
//
// Verified rather than assumed: stepping across cell 4096, where the mask rolls over,
// the largest density change is the same order as the same step taken at an ordinary
// cell plane. Measured the same way at shifts 8, 10 and 14 the wrap is likewise
// indistinguishable from an ordinary plane, so this is free to move.
//
// THE ASSET MUST BE PERIODIC. Repeat addressing is what closes the wrap, and it only
// closes it if the volume actually tiles -- generated from wrapped lattice hashes,
// not cropped out of a larger field. A non-tiling volume seams here, at a 4096-cell
// spacing, which is far enough apart to read as an unexplained occasional artifact
// rather than as an obvious tiling bug.
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_SHIFT 12
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK ((1 << UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_SHIFT) - 1)
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP ((float) (1 << UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_SHIFT))

// ONE TEXTURE, EVERY FETCH. The large warp octave used to read a separate curl volume
// so its three channels were guaranteed to be one coherent vector field rather than
// three unrelated archetypes at three scales -- an axis-aligned warp was the failure
// mode that guarded against:
//
//   large octave   NoiseTex, the packed Perlin-Worley
//   small octave   NoiseTex, the packed Perlin-Worley
//   region axes    NoiseTex
//   region axes    NoiseTex, second scale
//
// Measurement went the other way: the packed Perlin-Worley volume simply looked
// better as the large octave than the curl volume did, so it now reads NoiseTex like
// everything else, WarpTex is gone as a resource, and UNIVERSE_WARPTEX_SIGNED goes
// with it -- there is only one decode now, the same UNORM v*2-1 the small octave and
// the region fetches already used.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS. Curl noise is divergence-free by construction, so
// det(I + A*grad v) = 1 + A*div(v) + O(A^2) = 1 + O(A^2): the first-order volume
// change vanishes identically and the map folds only at second order. A generic field
// folds at first order and has no such guarantee. Measured against a generic
// three-channel field at matched gradient, fraction of samples with a negative
// Jacobian determinant:
//
//   shear     generic     curl
//    1.00      0.000%    0.000%
//    1.30      0.102%    0.007%
//    1.80      2.536%    0.565%
//    2.50     12.400%    4.750%
//
// Both octaves are now the generic column: the shear ceiling below is no longer a
// conservative bound with headroom above it, it is close to the actual tear point.
// Amplitudes tuned near the old ceiling are worth re-checking against this table.
//
// It also means void size is no longer purely VoidSizeSpread's job. A
// divergence-free warp cannot vary void size, which is why VoidSizeSpread was the
// only source of that variation; Perlin-Worley carries no such guarantee, so some of
// what reads as size variation now may be coming from the large octave instead. Not
// necessarily a problem -- may be exactly the organic read that was wanted -- but it
// is a second mechanism doing a job that used to have one, worth an eye if void size
// ever needs to be tuned independently of warp amplitude again.
//
// SWIZZLE TEST still applies as a diagnostic even without a per-octave asset choice
// to defend: read .yzx instead of .xyz for an octave and see whether the field's
// character rotates with it. If it does, that octave is axis-coupled.

// Fractional UV offsets, one per fetch. The two warp octaves are already decorrelated
// by their scale ratio, and now share a texture with the noise and region fetches, so
// these offsets mostly guard against a scale ratio someone has set to one and against
// every fetch that shares this asset landing on the same phase. The region offset is
// load-bearing for the same reason: the two region fetches read the SAME texture, and
// without a phase offset on top of their scale difference they would repeat each other's
// content. Whole numbers would be invisible under repeat addressing.

// ==========================================================================
// THE FETCH SCALES ARE FIXED, AND STAY FIXED
// ==========================================================================
// Both warp octaves are uniform across the draw and are NOT on the variance path. They were briefly, and were rolled
// back after looking at the result, so this section is a record of a thing that was
// tried rather than an argument from first principles alone.
//
// The UV is (cell & 4095 + frac) * scale, so a position-varying scale gives
//     d(uv)/dp = scale + base * d(scale)/dp
// and `base` runs to 4096. That second term is spurious frequency the octave is not
// supposed to have, and it is proportional to distance from the wrap origin. Measured
// on the large octave, base scale 0.05, region amplitude +-40%, as the gradient of
// the warp value per cell travelled against the same field with a fixed scale:
//
//   cells travelled       fixed     modulated      ratio
//         0 - 30          0.43         1.93         4.5x
//           100           0.45        11.36        25.2x
//         1 000           0.66       123.86       189.1x
//         3 000           1.31       725.49       554.3x
//
// It is not a near/far tradeoff with a usable near field -- it is wrong at the origin
// and gets worse, so anywhere it is tuned it is mistuned everywhere else. In practice
// the large octave stops reading as a large octave and becomes high-frequency noise,
// which is what the table says it should do and what it did.
//
// THE CLUSTER SCALE CAME BACK WITH THEM, and that closes an exposure rather than
// merely reverting a new one: the cluster stage's own scale had been resolved per sample
// into the identical UV construction since that path landed, so the same term was live
// on its fetch all along -- unremarked, because that fetch was grain rather than
// structure and the failure hides in it. That stage is gone now and every remaining
// scale is fixed and quantized once, so this class of error is out of the file entirely
// rather than confined to the pins someone remembered to leave alone. A new stage that
// wants a per-sample fetch frequency should read this section before authoring one.
//
// WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, if a region needs to look finer or coarser rather than merely
// more or less displaced: blend two fetches at two FIXED scales and let the region
// field drive the blend. Both scales stay constant so no spurious frequency appears,
// the blend's own gradient is a region-scale quantity and therefore tiny, and the
// perceptual result -- apparent scale moving with the region -- is the same. It costs
// one extra fetch per octave that wants it. That is the sound version, and it is what
// the rolled-back ranges were reaching for.

#define UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_X 0.317f
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Y 0.631f
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Z 0.149f

#define UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_X 0.743f
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_Y 0.281f
#define UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_Z 0.907f

#define UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_U 0.523f
#define UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_V 0.091f
#define UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_W 0.677f

// The second region field's own phase. Load-bearing for the same reason every other
// offset here is, and more so: A and B read the SAME texture, so without a phase
// difference on top of their scale difference the two would repeat each other's content
// at a stride set by the ratio of their scales.
#define UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_U 0.137f
#define UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_V 0.859f
#define UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_W 0.401f



struct UniverseDensityParams
{
    // --- WEB GEOMETRY ---
    // TWO LATTICES. The small one is the reference for everything else in this file:
    // every "per cell" unit -- warp amplitude and scale, both region scales,
    // feature width, void spread -- is per SMALL cell, and the large lattice inherits
    // all of them scaled by the ratio, so a region that switches scale switches scale
    // in every property at once rather than growing voids with unchanged wall widths.
    float InvCellSizeSmall; // small cells per normalized unit; larger = finer web
    // LARGE CELL SIZE IS QUANTIZED TO AN INTEGER MULTIPLE of the small one, and this
    // is precision rather than taste. The offset arrives as an exact integer count of
    // SMALL cells and has to be re-split into whole large cells plus a remainder; with
    // an integer ratio that is integer division and is exact at any magnitude, and with
    // an arbitrary ratio it is a float multiply against a count that reaches 1e11. At
    // that magnitude one ulp is hundreds of cells, and the error CHANGES as the offset
    // crosses a cell plane -- so the large lattice would shift bodily, discontinuously,
    // as the player moves. A tear, not a drift.
    //
    // The two lattices are therefore commensurate: large cell planes land on small cell
    // planes every LatticeRatio cells. That is worth an eye but should not show -- the
    // features live at bisectors between independently hashed NODES, not at cell
    // planes, and the warp displaces both. If a grid-aligned artifact ever appears at
    // exactly the ratio's spacing, this is where it comes from.
    float LatticeRatio; // large cell size / small, whole number, >= 1
    int LatticeRatioInt; // the same number, for the exact offset split
    // Only the COORDINATE SPLIT uses this now. The warp deliberately does not: its
    // amplitudes are carried into the coarse lattice as the same numbers rather than
    // the same physical displacement, so nothing is converted there. See the warp block
    // in SampleCell.
    float InvLatticeRatio; // 1 / LatticeRatio; converts small-cell units to large
    float LatticeLogRatioSq; // log2(ratio^2); the geometric lambda ramp's constant
    float CellSizeBias; // exponent shaping where between the two lattices a region sits

    // --- FIELD OFFSET, PRE-SPLIT ---
    // The player's position in the field, as an exact integer cell count plus a
    // fraction, and the SAME offset re-expressed in large cells. All of it is constant
    // for the draw, which is the whole point: the re-split is integer division, and it
    // used to run on every march step against inputs that never changed.
    //
    // LargeOffsetRem is the remainder of that division, carried as a float because that
    // is the only form SampleAtPosition wants it in -- it is added to a fractional cell
    // position and never used as an integer again.
    float3 OffsetFrac;
    int OffsetCellX;
    int OffsetCellY;
    int OffsetCellZ;
    int LargeOffsetCellX;
    int LargeOffsetCellY;
    int LargeOffsetCellZ;
    float LargeOffsetRemX;
    float LargeOffsetRemY;
    float LargeOffsetRemZ;
    float bLatticeLarge; // 0 when the ratio is 1 and the second walk is pure waste

    // --- WEB DENSITY AND WIDTH ---
    // What the march accumulates is density x volume fraction, not peak density. A
    // wall covers an order of magnitude more volume than a filament, so equal-looking
    // densities make the field read as foam. See MakeUniverseDensityParams.
    //
    // PACKED (min, max, bias, free) -- see VarianceRemap. There is no more single
    // default value nudged by a separate region amount: the region channel picks
    // where between the two authored extremes a given sample falls, and that IS the
    // value, not a multiplier on one. w is UNCLAIMED, a free per-field handle
    // reserved for whatever the next need turns out to be.
    //
    // WIDTH IS NOW ON THE VARIANCE PATH TOO -- it no longer rides density's
    // bias-shaped t as a derived coupling (the "wall is a slab" compounding note used
    // to live here). It reads the SAME channel as its paired density (x.y for wall,
    // x.z for filament) but resolves through its OWN bias/min/max, same pattern
    // WarpAmountLargeRange/WarpAmountSmallRange already use sharing the turbulence
    // channel: one raw signal, independent per-field response curves. The automatic
    // "wider feature needs less density for equal brightness" coupling is gone with
    // it -- if that compounded look is wanted, correlate the two ranges' authored
    // extremes by hand.
    //
    // NO LONGER PRECOMPUTABLE. InvWallWidth/InvFilamentWidth used to be struct
    // fields, computed once in MakeUniverseDensityParams; now that width is a
    // per-sample resolved value like density is, the reciprocal has to be taken
    // per-sample in SampleCell instead. One more division per sample, guarded the
    // same way every other division in this file is.
    //
    // WIDTH IS NOW A WEIGHT FALLOFF RATE, NOT A BAND EDGE. It used to be a hard cutoff
    // on a rank difference: past it, the feature contributed exactly nothing. It is now
    // the distance at which a competing node's weight has halved, which has no cutoff
    // at all -- the falloff is smooth and the tail is exponential rather than absent.
    // See WeightedNodeMeasure for the derivation of lambda from it.
    //
    // THERE IS ONLY ONE WIDTH NOW, and it is the filament's. Both features read the
    // same participant count off the same weights, so a second width would need a
    // second set of exponentials over the whole neighbourhood -- 27 more exp2 and 54
    // more madds -- to shift a threshold the wall term reaches by a cheaper route
    // anyway. FeatureWidthRange sets the lambda for both.
    //
    // WALL WIDTH IS NOW WALL FALLOFF, and this is a repurpose rather than a rename.
    // The wall term is saturate(N - 1) and 1 is the measure's own FLOOR, so the wall
    // is the feature that sits on top of the aggregate tail described at
    // WeightedNodeMeasure -- and once the tail alone carries N past 2, the saturate
    // pins the term at full and the old width pin stopped changing anything at all.
    // That is the symptom: a wall width that appears dead while filament width still
    // works, because the filament threshold at 2 sits above the tail rather than in it.
    // The exponent is the direct fix. It leaves the on-wall value at exactly 1, since
    // 1^p is 1, and crushes everything below: a tail sitting at 0.4 goes to 0.064 at
    // p = 3. Larger p is a faster transition to zero moving off the sheet into the
    // void, which is thickness control by a different lever, and it is also the tail
    // control the previous pass left outstanding. 1.0 reproduces the bare saturate.
    float4 WallDensityRange;
    float4 WallFalloffRange; // exponent on the wall term; >1 sharpens, <1 spreads
    float4 FilamentDensityRange;
    float4 FeatureWidthRange; // weight-halving distance, cells; sets the ONE lambda
    float4 VoidFloorRange; // ambient everywhere; keeps voids from being empty

    // THE SEED IS PRE-MIXED. CellHash used to fold the raw seed in with a multiply of
    // its own, which is loop-invariant and was therefore being paid on all fifty-four
    // candidates. Doing it once here costs nothing and removes a multiply from the
    // hottest loop in the file. The bits are identical -- it is the same product, hoisted.
    uint SeedHash;

    // --- ORGANICS ---
    // PACKED, same convention as the densities above.
    //
    // POWER-DIAGRAM OFFSET NOW, subtracted from the SQUARED distance rather than from
    // the distance. That is what the note in the old ranking loop used to say was
    // worth revisiting when the offset was next touched, and this is that time: the weight
    // wants a squared distance anyway, so the offset moved into that space and the 27
    // roots went with it. The unit changes with it -- see MakeUniverseDensityParams.
    float4 VoidSizeSpreadRange; // power-diagram offset per node, squared cell units
                                // .w CARRIES THE SKEW -- see below
    // VoidSizeSkew RIDES ITS OWN RANGE'S .w, which is the first of the free per-field
    // handles to be claimed. It is not independently ranged and never was: it already
    // rode VoidSizeSpreadRange's bias-shaped interpolant as a derived multiplier, so
    // the pin and the range were coupled in the field before they were coupled in the
    // struct. Carrying it in the range it is already a function of makes that explicit
    // and costs a pin. See the coupling note where mSkew is computed in SampleCell.
    float VoidSizeSkew; // >1 makes large voids rare and small ones common
    // Two octaves. Amounts are peak displacement in cells; scales are texture repeats
    // per CELL, quantized to 1/UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP so the precision wrap stays seamless.
    // Shear -- what actually curves the web -- is amount * scale * gradient, so no pin
    // here means anything on its own and the fold ceiling couples all five.
    // PACKED, same convention: no more base amount, just the two authored extremes.
    //
    // EACH SCALE RIDES ITS OWN OCTAVE'S AMOUNT RANGE, in .w. The two are the same
    // octave and multiply together in the shear product that decides whether the web
    // tears, so a pin that carried one without the other was always half a setting --
    // and the scale is a single fixed number (see THE FETCH SCALES ARE FIXED above),
    // which is exactly what a .w handle holds. Quantized once in
    // MakeUniverseDensityParams, into the two scalars below.
    float4 WarpAmountLargeRange; // .xyz displacement in cells; .w repeats per cell
    float WarpScaleLarge; // unpacked from WarpAmountLargeRange.w and quantized
    float4 WarpAmountSmallRange; // .xyz breaks up the geometry; .w repeats per cell
    float WarpScaleSmall; // unpacked from WarpAmountSmallRange.w and quantized
    // Per-axis gain on the decoded warp displacement, one vector per octave. Unlike a
    // scalar site's weights (dot-product down to one value), a warp needs all three
    // axes, so the weight is an elementwise multiply on the decoded (x,y,z) before
    // they become the displacement -- w is unused, since there is no fourth axis to
    // steer. Set a component to 0 to flatten the warp along that axis, negative to
    // invert it, |w|>1 for gain.
    //
    // WarpLargeWeights.w IS NO LONGER UNUSED. It carries how much of the cell size ratio
    // the large octave's AMPLITUDE follows on the coarse lattice: 0 for the same physical
    // displacement on both tiers, 1 for the same displacement measured in each tier's own
    // cells. Saturated, so an out-of-range value clamps rather than doing something
    // strange to a vector that is otherwise deliberately unclamped. See the warp block in
    // SampleCell. WarpSmallWeights.w remains unused -- the small octave is pinned at full
    // follow, which is what "scales its amount" means for it.
    float4 WarpLargeWeights;
    float4 WarpSmallWeights;

    // TWO VARIANCE FETCHES, EIGHT CHANNELS, and the second one exists because four was
    // a hard ceiling rather than a tight fit. Every consumer resolves its own bias and
    // its own min/max off its channel's raw value, but a bias only reshapes a response
    // curve -- two fields on one channel remain monotone functions of one scalar, so
    // their rank correlation is exactly 1 whatever the biases are. Authoring a range
    // backwards flips the sign, which means a same-channel pair can traverse either
    // DIAGONAL of its 2x2 but never reach the off-diagonal corners.
    //
    // Nor can a fifth axis be manufactured from four channels. Four independent channels
    // span a four-dimensional space and any combination of them is a point in that same
    // space: x*y correlates 0.65 with x, x-y correlates 0.71 with x. Combinations rotate
    // within the space, they do not extend it. With one fetch the reachable parameter set
    // was a four-dimensional surface in a thirteen-dimensional space, and since
    // distinguishable variety goes as levels^dimensions, four to eight axes is not a
    // doubling of the possible universes but roughly a squaring.
    //
    // THE SPLIT IS STRUCTURE AGAINST APPEARANCE, at two different scales.
    //
    //   FIELD A, COARSE -- what shape the web has
    //     A.x  which lattice (the cell-size blend)
    //     A.y  void size spread, and the skew that shapes its distribution
    //     A.z  feature width, the single lambda for walls and filaments alike
    //     A.w  large warp octave amount
    //
    //   FIELD B, FINER -- how it is painted, plus local grain
    //     B.x  wall density
    //     B.y  wall falloff
    //     B.z  filament density, void floor
    //     B.w  small warp octave amount
    //
    // WHAT IS DELIBERATELY STILL CORRELATED. Spread with skew, because they are one
    // setting: skew distributes the offsets that spread scales. Filament density with the
    // floor, because how much haze a region carries reasonably tracks how much structure
    // it has.
    //
    // B.w AND THE SECOND HALF OF B.z ARE FREE now that the cluster/gas stage is out, so
    // the next consumer to arrive has somewhere uncorrelated to go without a third fetch.
    //
    // WHAT WAS PULLED APART, and why each mattered. The two warp octaves, which shared a
    // channel while the file claimed independent amplitudes delivered "this region is
    // macro-warped, that one is micro-warped" -- unreachable on one channel, and the two
    // octaves have since turned out to do genuinely different jobs. The lattice blend
    // from void spread, which locked the two largest structural knobs together so that
    // large uniform voids and small varied ones were both impossible. Feature width from
    // filament density, which was an accident of the name width used to have. Wall
    // density from wall falloff, brightness from sharpness.
    //
    // WHAT IS ALREADY RIGHT AND SHOULD STAY: wall density and filament density on
    // different channels. Sheet-dominated against strand-dominated is the most
    // characterful variation this layer has.
    //
    // ONE LIMIT THE ALLOCATION CANNOT FIX: the packed asset has four archetypes, so B
    // reuses A's four at a different frequency. The structure differs, the archetype
    // pairings repeat. Probably invisible; worth knowing before blaming something else.
    //
    // TWO SCALES, and the difference is not decoration. Two fetches at the SAME scale
    // would give independent values on a shared structure -- same period, boundaries in
    // the same places -- which is precisely the failure the retired hash lattice had and
    // which the phase offsets alone do not fix. Different scales make A's provinces and
    // B's patches fall in different places, so a region reads as one place with internal
    // variety rather than as everything switching at one boundary.
    //
    // KEEP THEM OFF A SIMPLE RATIO, with each other AND with both warp scales. That is
    // six pairs to check now rather than one, and the quantization makes the check exact
    // rather than a matter of judgement: every scale is a whole number of 1/4096ths, so
    // two of them re-align every 4096/gcd(numerators) cells. Pick numerators that are
    // MUTUALLY COPRIME and the only re-alignment is at the wrap itself, which is already
    // a seam. Odd numerators are coprime to any power-of-two scale for free.
    //
    // Worked, at the shipped defaults: 53/4096 = 0.012939 (77 cells) for A, 119/4096 =
    // 0.029053 (34 cells) for B, against warp octaves at 205/4096 and 2048/4096. 53, 119
    // and 205 are pairwise coprime and all three are odd, so no pair of the four repeats
    // before 4096 cells. The failure this avoids is the one the old note describes: 1/32
    // against 0.05 is 8:5 and re-aligns every 160 cells, close enough to walk across.
    float RegionScaleA; // structure field, coarse; quantized like the warp
    float RegionScaleB; // appearance field, finer; quantized like the warp
    float bWarpEnable; // skips the warp fetch entirely when the warp is off
    float bRegionEnableA; // skips fetch A when every structure range is degenerate
    float bRegionEnableB; // skips fetch B when every appearance range is degenerate

    // --- BOUNDS ---
    // The field itself is unbounded and tiles forever through the cell hash. This
    // fade exists only to keep a finite proxy from cutting it off with a hard edge.
    // Set BoundsFadeStart >= 1 to disable.
    // --- MARCH ---
    // Not used inside this file at all; it is derived here because this is where the
    // cell size and the feature width both live, and handed to the march. See
    // UNIVERSE_MIN_STEP_FRACTION.
    float MinFeatureStep; // normalized units; the step below which sampling is wasted

    float BoundsFadeStart;
    float BoundsFadeStartSq;
    float InvBoundsFadeSpan;


    // ======================================================================
    // HELPERS
    // ======================================================================

    // Raises a hash value in [0,1) to the skew exponent. With UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED set
    // this is repeated squaring and the exponent argument is ignored, which is what
    // makes the regional skew modulation a no-op in that configuration -- deliberate,
    // and the reason RegionVoidScale drives spread alone unless the general path is
    // switched back on.
    float SkewPow(float x, float p)
    {
#if UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED == 2
        return x * x;
#elif UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED == 4
        float x2 = x * x;
        return x2 * x2;
#elif UNIVERSE_SKEW_FIXED == 8
        float x2 = x * x;
        float x4 = x2 * x2;
        return x4 * x4;
#else
        return PowSafe(x, p);
#endif
    }

    // Guards the BASE only. pow(0, p) is undefined for p <= 0 and the compilers
    // disagree about what it returns; the exponent is deliberately unguarded, so a
    // negative concentration or skew will blow up near the profile edge and that is
    // visible rather than absorbed.
    float PowSafe(float x, float p)
    {
        return (x > UNIVERSE_POW_EPSILON) ? pow(x, p) : 0.0f;
    }

    // Two conventions for turning one raw texture fetch into what a sample site
    // needs, both centring the UNORM fetch to [-1,1] first. A WARP OCTAVE needs three
    // axes, not one number, so its weights are an elementwise GAIN on the decoded
    // channels -- w has nowhere to go, since there is no fourth displacement axis.
    // The scalar-site convention that used to sit beside this -- four channels collapsed
    // with a dot product -- went out with the cluster/gas stage, its only consumer. It is
    // worth restoring rather than reinventing if a new stage needs one weighted number
    // out of a fetch: positive takes the channel, negative inverts it, |w| > 1 is gain.
    float3 WeightedVector3(float4 RawSample, float4 InWeights)
    {
        float4 c = (RawSample * 2.0f - 1.0f) * InWeights;
        return c.xyz;
    }

    // THE VARIANCE CONVENTION: every packed (min, max, bias, free) field is resolved
    // in two steps, split apart because some quantities need the intermediate t --
    // the width/skew couplings in SampleCell reuse it as a signed delta rather than
    // re-deriving their own shape from the raw channel.
    //
    // VarianceT is the RAW CHANNEL only, never centred -- unlike WeightedScalar's
    // [-1,1] convention, a lerp wants its blend factor in [0,1] to begin with, so
    // there is no centring step here at all. saturate guards the pow from a channel
    // that drifts fractionally outside [0,1] under filtering; PowSafe guards it from
    // a zero base under a non-positive bias.
    float VarianceT(float InChannel, float InBias)
    {
        return PowSafe(saturate(InChannel), InBias);
    }

    // Then a plain lerp between the two authored extremes. This IS the value now --
    // not a multiplier nudging some other default -- so a range of (5, 5, 1, 0)
    // reproduces a constant exactly the way the old single-scalar pins did.
    float VarianceRemap(float InT, float4 InRange)
    {
        return lerp(InRange.x, InRange.y, InT);
    }


    // PCG-style integer hash. uint arithmetic wraps identically in C++ and HLSL, so
    // this is bit-exact across both -- which is what lets the CPU place objects at
    // the same nodes the shader draws. Returns four values in [0,1): xyz is the
    // node's offset within its cell, w is its additive radius offset.
    float4 CellHash(int InX, int InY, int InZ)
    {
        // Cast to uint BEFORE multiplying. Signed overflow is undefined behaviour in
        // C++ -- not merely implementation-defined -- and a compiler that can prove it
        // happens is free to fold the whole expression away. Observed: gcc -O2 warns
        // -Waggressive-loop-optimizations and collapses this hash to a constant, which
        // renders as a field with no structure at all. Unsigned overflow wraps by
        // definition in both languages and matches the GPU's two's-complement result
        // bit for bit, which is the entire point of hashing here rather than on the
        // CPU alone. Suffix every multiplier with u so HLSL cannot type one as int.
        uint ux = (uint) InX;
        uint uy = (uint) InY;
        uint uz = (uint) InZ;

        uint n = (ux * 73856093u) ^ (uy * 19349663u) ^ (uz * 83492791u);
        n ^= SeedHash;

#if UNIVERSE_FAST_HASH
        n ^= n >> 16u;
        n *= 0x7feb352du;
        n ^= n >> 15u;
        n *= 0x846ca68bu;
        n ^= n >> 16u;

        const float Inv255 = 1.0f / 255.0f;
        return float4(float(n & 255u) * Inv255,
                      float((n >> 8u) & 255u) * Inv255,
                      float((n >> 16u) & 255u) * Inv255,
                      float((n >> 24u) & 255u) * Inv255);
#else
        uint a = n * 1664525u + 1013904223u;
        uint b = a * 1664525u + 1013904223u;
        uint c = b * 1664525u + 1013904223u;
        uint d = c * 1664525u + 1013904223u;

        a ^= a >> 16u;
        b ^= b >> 16u;
        c ^= c >> 16u;
        d ^= d >> 16u;
        a *= 1664525u;
        b *= 1664525u;
        c *= 1664525u;
        d *= 1664525u;
        a ^= a >> 16u;
        b ^= b >> 16u;
        c ^= c >> 16u;
        d ^= d >> 16u;

        const float Inv32 = 1.0f / 4294967296.0f;
        return float4(float(a) * Inv32, float(b) * Inv32,
                      float(c) * Inv32, float(d) * Inv32);
#endif
    }

    // The four regional axes, each centred on zero and scaled by its own amount.
    //
    // WHAT MAY BE MODULATED, and why the line falls where it does.
    //
    // Anything consumed AFTER the ranking -- the two densities, the two feature
    // widths, the floor -- is free. It changes how the skeleton is
    // painted, never where the skeleton is, so a Voronoi vertex the CPU constructs is
    // still the vertex the shader draws.
    //
    // Anything consumed INSIDE the ranking -- spread and skew -- moves the
    // skeleton itself. That is allowed here, because the modulation is evaluated once
    // per sample and every one of the 27 nodes then shares it, so each sample still
    // sees a consistent tessellation and the field stays continuous. What it costs is
    // exactness: the tessellation is now a slowly varying family rather than one
    // fixed diagram, so a vertex constructed at a point uses the modulation at that
    // point and the shader a cell away uses a very slightly different one. Over a
    // 32-cell period against a 1-cell neighbourhood that discrepancy is negligible.
    // It stops being negligible if the regional period is ever brought near the cell
    // size, and the failure is subtle -- placement drifting off the drawn filament --
    // so the period and this modulation are coupled and should move together.
    //
    // WarpAmountLargeRange/SmallRange ALSO GET A CUT of this fetch now, on channel x
    // -- see the struct field comment above RegionScale for why that trade was made
    // and what it costs.
    //
    // NEITHER CELL SIZE may be modulated, and this is not a matter of degree. A cell
    // size is what converts a position into a cell index, so varying it spatially makes
    // the lattice non-uniform: neighbouring samples disagree about which cell they are
    // in, the int/frac split stops being a partition, and the field tears rather than
    // bends. Both lattices are fixed across the draw for that reason.
    //
    // WHAT THE REGION FIELD MOVES INSTEAD is which of the two lattices a sample reads,
    // through the a.w blend -- see TWO LATTICES, ONE BLEND. That is the sound version
    // of "cell size varies regionally," and it is why this restriction stopped being a
    // dead end.
    // ONE FETCH, FOUR CHANNELS, and that is sufficient precisely because the asset is
    // packed with a different archetype per channel. The hash lattice this replaced
    // could not do it: its four channels were the same trilinear value noise on one
    // lattice differing only in seed, so their VALUES were independent -- measured at
    // +-0.005 pairwise, with all sixteen sign combinations occupied to within 6.1-6.4%
    // of uniform -- but their STRUCTURE was shared. Same period, same phase, same
    // quintic envelope zeroing at the same planes, which showed up as a uniform +0.17
    // correlation between every pair of gradient magnitudes. All four axes therefore
    // transitioned in the same places, and a region was a region for all four
    // properties at once.
    //
    // Four archetypes at four scales, decided at bake time, dissolves that for free.
    // Four fetches at four runtime scales would too, and would cost four fetches --
    // which is why the second region field is a second fetch at a different SCALE
    // rather than four more runtime-scaled channels.
    //
    // ASSUMES THE CHANNELS ARE NORMALIZED, centred at 0.5 with comparable spread.
    // Different archetypes have genuinely different histograms -- worley F1 is not
    // uniform, ridged is not centred -- and if they arrive raw then a Range's min and
    // max stop landing where they're expected, one axis saturating while another
    // barely moves. Normalizing at bake keeps a channel meaning the same spread of
    // "how far toward this axis's max" whichever channel it lands on and whichever
    // asset is loaded; the alternative is a per-channel centre and scale travelling
    // with the asset, which is the same information carried worse.
    //
    // AMPLITUDES ONLY, NEVER THE SCALES, for the warp-turbulence output on x -- and
    // this is a hard constraint of the UV construction rather than a preference. The
    // UV is (cell & 4095 + frac) * scale, so a position-varying scale gives
    // d(uv)/dp = scale + base * d(scale)/dp with `base` reaching 4096. Measured on
    // the large octave at base scale 0.05 with a +-40% region field, the warp's
    // spatial gradient multiplies by 751x at the shipped wrap. The large octave stops
    // being a large octave and becomes high-frequency noise. Nothing is lost by
    // restricting to amplitude: independent large and small AMPLITUDES already
    // deliver "this region is macro-warped, that one is micro-warped."
    //
    // NO PER-CHANNEL PROCESSING HAPPENS HERE ANY MORE. This used to centre each
    // channel and scale it by its own RegionXVariance amount before returning; both
    // steps moved to the call sites, since every consumer is now a packed Range with
    // its own bias and its own min/max, via VarianceT/VarianceRemap. This function is
    // just the fetch.
    float4 RegionAxes(Texture3D InTex, SamplerState InSampler,
                      int InCellX, int InCellY, int InCellZ, float3 InFrac,
                      float InScale, float3 InPhase)
    {
#if UNIVERSE_COST_PROBE >= 1
        // Neutral midpoint, not zero -- a disabled/probed-out fetch should read as
        // "the middle of every authored range," matching the same fallback used when
        // bRegionEnable is off at runtime.
        return float4(0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f);
#else
        // Same masked cell-relative construction as the warp, and exact for the same
        // reason. Both region scales are quantized so the 4096-cell wrap closes
        // seamlessly. Scale and phase are arguments rather than struct reads because
        // this is called TWICE per sample now, once per region field.
        float3 uv = float3(
            (float(InCellX & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + InFrac.x) * InScale + InPhase.x,
            (float(InCellY & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + InFrac.y) * InScale + InPhase.y,
            (float(InCellZ & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + InFrac.z) * InScale + InPhase.z);

        return InTex.SampleLevel(InSampler, uv, 0.0f);
#endif
    }

    // ======================================================================
    // THE WEB
    // ======================================================================

    // THE EFFECTIVE PARTICIPANT COUNT, over the 3x3x3 neighbourhood of the cell
    // containing InFrac. This function returns one lattice's contribution to it as
    // (sum of weights, sum of squared weights); the caller adds the two lattices'
    // returns together and divides. Returns a pair rather than taking out parameters:
    // HLSL's `out` has no C++ equivalent, and a reference parameter would have to be
    // spelled differently in each language -- exactly the kind of divergence the shared
    // file exists to prevent.
    //
    // The measure the caller builds from these is
    //
    //     p_i = w_i / sum_j w_j        N = 1 / sum_i p_i^2 = (sum w)^2 / (sum w^2)
    //
    // which is 1 where one node dominates, 2 where two tie, 3 where three do, k where k
    // tie. It answers "how many nodes are simultaneously nearest" directly rather than
    // by sorting and differencing, and it is smooth rather than merely continuous:
    // there is no rank swap left to kink the gradient, because there are no ranks.
    //
    // BOTH LATTICES FEED ONE SUM, which is what makes this a single tessellation rather
    // than two fields laid over each other -- see TWO LATTICES, ONE DIAGRAM. The
    // consequence for this function is that everything reaching it must already be in
    // the SHARED metric: InDistScale converts a lattice's own squared cell units into
    // small-cell units, and InUBias carries the suppression that decides which lattice
    // owns the territory. Neither is a property of a node; both are uniform over the
    // walk, so the tessellation a sample sees is consistent across all fifty-four
    // candidates and the field stays continuous.
    //
    // WHY THE MEASURE NEEDS NO NORMALIZATION. (sum w)^2 / (sum w^2) is invariant under
    // scaling every weight by a common factor, so the distribution never has to be
    // divided through and the nearest distance never has to be found to anchor it. One
    // pass, two accumulators, no minimum, no branches, and both accumulators are plain
    // sums -- reassociable, so the fully unrolled 27 carries no serial dependency at
    // all. The old insertion chain carried three.
    //
    // THE WEIGHT. exp2(lambda * (pivot - u)), where u is the POWER-DIAGRAM squared
    // distance in SMALL-CELL units
    //
    //     u = ( |node - sample|^2 - offset ) * distScale  +  uBias
    //
    // and the offset is the skewed hash channel scaled by the resolved spread. The
    // spread rides inside the distScale multiply deliberately: a lattice's void-size
    // variation should be the same FRACTION of its own cells on both lattices, so the
    // coarse one's offsets scale up with its cells rather than staying a fixed absolute
    // size and doing almost nothing at ratio 4.
    //
    // Three things fall out of choosing this form:
    //
    //   NO ROOTS. The weight wants a squared distance, and the offset lives in squared
    //   space with it, so there are no sqrt calls in the walk at all. exp2 replaces the
    //   root that the ranked formulation needed at the same rate, so the loop is net
    //   cheaper by the whole insertion chain.
    //
    //   BOUNDED REACH. The old offset was subtracted from the DISTANCE and had no bound
    //   relative to the search window: at spread 0.80 a node one cell outside the span
    //   beat every node inside it, which misidentified the nearest node on 8.66% of
    //   samples and made the diagram itself wrong there. Subtracting from the SQUARE
    //   bounds it -- a node out-reaches an unoffset one by exactly `spread` in squared
    //   distance, full stop.
    //
    //   ONE EXPONENTIAL, FULL STOP. Per candidate the whole cost is a hash, three
    //   subtracts, three madds for the squared distance, the skew's two multiplies, one
    //   exp2 and two madds for the accumulators.
    //
    // LAMBDA, PIVOT AND SUPPRESSION ARE THE CALLER'S, not this function's, because both
    // lattices have to share them for their weights to be comparable. Their derivations
    // are at UNIVERSE_WIDTH_TO_LAMBDA and in SampleCell.
    //
    // WIDTH IS NO LONGER UNIFORM ACROSS THE FIELD, and this is the one behavioural
    // change from the ranked formulation worth watching for rather than tuning around.
    // Two nodes at distance d either side of a wall differ in u by 4*d*x for an offset
    // x, so the apparent linear width is 1/(4*lambda*d) -- inversely proportional to how
    // far apart the two nodes are. Walls bounding a large void read THINNER than walls
    // bounding a small one at the same authored width, and that now applies ACROSS the
    // two lattices as well as within one: the coarse lattice's sheets are thinner
    // relative to its voids than the fine lattice's are. Whether that reads as a defect
    // or as exactly the right "big voids have thin sheets" behaviour is a judgement to
    // make on screen.
    //
    // THE TAIL IS THE OTHER REAL COST, and it is not the second-nearest node -- it is
    // the AGGREGATE of the candidates that are not in contention. Each contributes a
    // weight ratio r_i and the measure reads about 1 + sum(r_i) in a void interior. With
    // fifty-four candidates rather than twenty-seven there is twice as much of it in
    // principle, but only in principle: the losing lattice is suppressed or outranged by
    // an exponential, so through the interior of any region its whole contribution is
    // below float resolution and only the transition bands carry both. WallFalloffRange
    // is the lever on what remains.
    //
    // TRUNCATION. Sorted order statistics of a fixed set were already continuous; the
    // jumps came from the SET changing at a cell plane. At the instant of a transition
    // the departing and arriving candidates both sit at squared distance >= 1.0 from the
    // sample in that lattice's own units -- the sample is on the face of its own cell
    // and the swapped slab starts one full cell out -- so their weight relative to the
    // dominant one is at most
    //
    //     2^( -lambda * distScale * (1.0 - spread - u1) )
    //
    // with u1 the dominant candidate's own power distance in the same units. At a wall
    // width of 0.06, which is lambda 53, a spread of 0.15 and a typical u1 of 0.2, that
    // is 2^-34 on the fine lattice and smaller still on the coarse one, whose distScale
    // multiplies the exponent. The old formulation swapped a rank outright and stepped
    // by a finite amount.
    //
    // Measured, on the single-lattice configuration. Density difference across a cell
    // plane against the same difference taken mid-cell, same step, 12k straddling pairs
    // -- so the interior column is the ordinary local slope and anything above it at the
    // plane is truncation:
    //
    //                        AT A CELL PLANE                  MID-CELL
    //                     p99      p99.9      max          p99       max
    //   spread 0.10
    //     ranked        0.0039    0.0146    0.0179        0.0032    0.0151
    //     weighted      0.0036    0.0155    0.0293        0.0029    0.0209
    //   spread 0.40
    //     ranked        0.0044    0.4900    1.5900        0.0031    0.0153
    //     weighted      0.0036    0.0164    0.0330        0.0025    0.0332
    //   spread 0.80
    //     ranked        0.5380    1.5400    3.7300        0.0031    0.0168
    //     weighted      0.0036    0.0954    1.9900        0.0028    0.0174
    //
    // At the spread the ranked field was shipped with, ONE PERCENT of cell-plane
    // crossings stepped by half the feature amplitude and a thousandth of them by more
    // than the whole of it -- against a field whose peak is about 4. The weighted field
    // is indistinguishable from mid-cell out to p99 at every spread tested. It is not
    // perfect: the tail at 0.80 still reaches a full-amplitude step on a handful of
    // crossings, because a spread that large genuinely does put the true nearest node
    // outside the window and no reweighting invents data that was never fetched.
    //
    // The inequality is the thing to keep an eye on, not the numbers: it degrades with
    // WIDE features (small lambda) and with LARGE spread. A wall widened to 0.25 cells
    // drops lambda to 12.8 and the bound to 2^-8, still negligible; it is spread that
    // runs out first, and past about 0.8 the honest fix is a wider search rather than a
    // smaller number here.
    float2 LatticeWeightSums(int InCellX, int InCellY, int InCellZ, float3 InFrac,
                             float InLambda, float InPivot,
                             float InResolvedSpread, float InSkewScale,
                             float InDistScale, float InUBias)
    {
        float sumW = 0.0f;
        float sumSqW = 0.0f;

#if UNIVERSE_COST_PROBE >= 3
        // Probe level 3: one cell, not twenty-seven. The field is not merely
        // meaningless here, it is EMPTY -- a single candidate per lattice gives N = 1
        // identically once the two sums are combined, and both features read as void.
        // The point is the instruction count with the unrolled search removed.
        for (int oz = 0; oz <= 0; oz++)
        {
            for (int oy = 0; oy <= 0; oy++)
            {
                for (int ox = 0; ox <= 0; ox++)
                {
#else
        for (int oz = -1; oz <= 1; oz++)
        {
            for (int oy = -1; oy <= 1; oy++)
            {
                for (int ox = -1; ox <= 1; ox++)
                {
#endif
                    float4 h = CellHash(InCellX + ox, InCellY + oy, InCellZ + oz);

                    // Node position relative to the sample, in this lattice's cell
                    // units.
                    float dx = float(ox) + h.x - InFrac.x;
                    float dy = float(oy) + h.y - InFrac.y;
                    float dz = float(oz) + h.z - InFrac.z;

                    // POWER-DIAGRAM OFFSET: subtracted from the square, so a node with a
                    // larger offset claims more space and voids differ in size.
                    //
                    // The skew is what mixes SCALES rather than merely jittering one
                    // scale. A uniform offset distribution gives every void a size near
                    // the mean; the exponent makes a large offset rare, and a node whose
                    // offset far exceeds its neighbours' swallows their territory
                    // outright, leaving one wide void where several small ones would
                    // have been. Removing a node by geometry this way is continuous,
                    // unlike a hash threshold that deletes it -- which is the same
                    // mechanism the lattice suppression below uses, at the scale of a
                    // whole lattice rather than one node.
                    //
                    // Not re-centred on zero: the measure is invariant under a common
                    // scaling of every weight, and a constant added to every u is
                    // exactly such a scaling, so centring would cost a subtract to
                    // change nothing at all.
                    float u = (dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz
                             - SkewPow(h.w, VoidSizeSkew * InSkewScale)
                               * InResolvedSpread) * InDistScale
                            + InUBias;

                    // exp2 rather than exp: it is the hardware primitive on both sides,
                    // and folding ln(2) into lambda would only move the multiply.
                    //
                    // THE CLAMP IS AN OVERFLOW GUARD and belongs here rather than in the
                    // pivot. Bounding the exponent by lowering the pivot was tried and
                    // does not survive two lattices: the lowest u a candidate can reach
                    // is -spread * ratio^2, and at ratio 3 that sits so far below the
                    // TYPICAL u that a pivot able to cover both underflows every weight
                    // in the field to zero and returns pure void. Clamping the exponent
                    // instead leaves the pivot free to track where the dominant
                    // candidate actually is, which is the only place it can usefully be.
                    //
                    // It caps a single weight at 2^55 and the squared accumulator's
                    // fifty-four-term sum at 2^116, so overflow is impossible for any
                    // parameter combination -- and overflow here produces a NaN the
                    // march spreads down the whole ray. It bites only for a candidate
                    // already dominating by more than sixteen orders of magnitude,
                    // which needs a node with the full offset sitting essentially on top
                    // of the sample.
                    float w = exp2(min(InLambda * (InPivot - u),
                                       UNIVERSE_EXP_HEADROOM));

                    sumW += w;
                    sumSqW += w * w;
                }
            }
        }

        return float2(sumW, sumSqW);
    }

    // Density from the participant count.
    //
    // A wall is where two nodes participate and a filament is where three do, so both
    // features are the SAME count, shifted to a different threshold and clamped. There
    // is no band edge and no normalized coordinate: the falloff shape comes from the
    // weight exponential upstream.
    //
    // THE NESTING IS PRESERVED, by the same mechanism as before but more directly. A
    // point on a filament has three participants, so the wall term -- which asks for
    // two -- saturates at full rather than falling off, and the filament term adds on
    // top of a wall term already at maximum. That is what makes a filament read
    // brighter than a bare wall without a separate term fighting it for brightness, and
    // it is what the saturates are for; they are not clamping an out-of-range value.
    //
    // THE WALL EXPONENT IS WHERE THE TAIL IS PAID FOR. The two thresholds are not
    // symmetric: the filament's sits at 2, comfortably above the measure's floor, so
    // the aggregate tail described at WeightedNodeMeasure never reaches it and
    // saturate(N - 2) is genuinely zero through a void interior. The wall's sits at 1,
    // which IS the floor, so the wall term reads the tail directly -- and once the tail
    // alone carries N past 2 the saturate pins it at full and no amount of retuning the
    // upstream rate moves it. That asymmetry is why the wall gets an exponent and the
    // filament does not.
    //
    // The exponent leaves the sheet itself untouched -- on a wall N is 2, the base is
    // exactly 1, and 1^p is 1 for every p -- and compresses everything below it. A tail
    // sitting at 0.4 goes to 0.16 at p = 2 and 0.064 at p = 3. So it is simultaneously
    // the thickness control the width pin used to be and the void-clearing control the
    // previous pass left outstanding, and 1.0 recovers the bare saturate exactly.
    //
    // PowSafe rather than pow: the base reaches zero across most of the field and
    // pow(0, p) is undefined for non-positive p, with the two compilers disagreeing
    // about what it returns. The exponent stays unguarded, so a negative falloff blows
    // up visibly rather than being absorbed -- same convention as every other exponent
    // in this file.
    //
    // WHAT SATURATE COSTS. It puts a corner in the field wherever the count crosses the
    // clamp -- along the filaments, for the wall term. The old profile had a corner at
    // its band edge instead, so this is a corner moved rather than a corner introduced,
    // but it has moved onto the brightest structure in the field rather than sitting at
    // its faint outer edge. An exponent above 1 flattens the approach to that corner,
    // so raising the falloff softens it as a side effect; if it still shows, a
    // smoothstep in place of the saturate costs two instructions.
    //
    // CLUSTERS ARE AVAILABLE HERE and are deliberately not taken: a vertex is where
    // four nodes participate, so saturate(N - 3.0f) off the same measure would be the
    // geometric cluster the F4 rank used to provide, for one instruction. There is no
    // cluster stage at all at the moment, so whatever replaces it starts from a clean
    // choice between this and something new.
    //
    // InResolvedDensity is (wall, filament); the falloff and the floor are likewise
    // fully resolved off their packed ranges by the caller. Width does not appear here
    // at all -- it is consumed upstream as the weight falloff rate.
    float DensityFromWeb(float InMeasure, float2 InResolvedDensity,
                         float InResolvedWallFalloff, float InResolvedVoidFloor)
    {
        // --- WALLS: two nodes participating, a face ---
        float wall = PowSafe(saturate(InMeasure - 1.0f), InResolvedWallFalloff);

        // --- FILAMENTS: three nodes participating, an edge ---
        float filament = saturate(InMeasure - 2.0f);

        return InResolvedDensity.x * wall
             + InResolvedDensity.y * filament
             + InResolvedVoidFloor;
    }

    // NOT APPLIED BY SampleAtPosition. It used to be, and that was wrong: this is a
    // property of how the field is BEING VIEWED, not of the field, and the only caller
    // that has any business with it is the ray march -- which is also the only one that
    // knows about the bounding sphere it fades toward.
    //
    // Entity generation samples the same field and must see none of this. A bounds fade
    // in the placement path would make where an entity lands depend on where the player
    // happened to be standing when the cell was generated, which is exactly the class of
    // view-dependence the cell/frac coordinate design exists to prevent.
    float BoundsFade(float rBoundsSq)
    {
        if (rBoundsSq <= BoundsFadeStartSq)
        {
            return 1.0f;
        }

        float t = (sqrt(rBoundsSq) - BoundsFadeStart) * InvBoundsFadeSpan;
        return 1.0f - t * t * (3.0f - 2.0f * t);
    }

    // Cell-relative entry point. This is the one that survives virtual traversal:
    // the integer part carries magnitude, the fraction carries precision, and
    // neither ever has to hold 1e11 in a float32.
    //
    // The warp displaces the FRACTION and re-normalizes the split, so the displaced
    // sample is still addressed as an exact integer cell plus a fraction in [0,1).
    // Displacing a reconstructed float3 position would work for a few thousand cells
    // and then quietly stop resolving.
    // The march calls this with the regional axes it fetched ONCE for the whole ray.
    //
    // Hoisting them out is the cheapest large saving available: the regional lattice
    // is 32 cells across and the proxy is 8, so a ray never spans more than a quarter
    // of one lattice cell and holding the axes constant along it changes the field by
    // less than the dither. It was costing 8 hashes on every one of 256 steps to
    // resolve a variation that cannot occur within a single ray.
    // Takes the texture and sampler because the warp is a fetch. Threaded through
    // rather than held as a struct member: HLSL resource types in a struct that is
    // also constructed as a local are a portability problem, and the galaxy core
    // settled the same question the same way.
    float SampleCell(Texture3D InTex, SamplerState InSampler,
                     int InCellX, int InCellY, int InCellZ, float3 InFrac,
                     int InLargeX, int InLargeY, int InLargeZ, float3 InLargeFrac)
    {
        int cx = InCellX;
        int cy = InCellY;
        int cz = InCellZ;
        float3 frac = InFrac;

        // The large lattice's own exact split, decomposed by the caller. Everything
        // fetch-related below -- both region fields and the warp -- is anchored to the SMALL
        // lattice and shared; only the neighbourhood walk happens twice.
        int lx = InLargeX;
        int ly = InLargeY;
        int lz = InLargeZ;
        float3 lfrac = InLargeFrac;

        float3 warpDelta = float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);

        // THE REGIONAL FETCH IS PER SAMPLE, at the sample's own position, and it used
        // to be hoisted to one fetch per ray taken at the ray's ENTRY POINT on the
        // bounding sphere. That was wrong in a way the cost argument for it obscured.
        //
        // The defence was that a ray crosses at most a quarter of one region, so
        // holding the axes constant along it changes little. True, and beside the
        // point: it bounds how much the region varies ALONG a ray while saying nothing
        // about the anchor. The entry point can be eight cells from the sample against
        // a thirty-four cell period, so the value used was up to a quarter-period away
        // from where it belonged -- and because the proxy is pegged to the camera, that
        // point travels through field space as the player moves. The modulation slid
        // across the field instead of being part of it. A property of the view, not of
        // the universe.
        //
        // Sampled BEFORE the warp, so it is anchored to unwarped field space -- which
        // is also required, since the turbulence axis modulates the warp itself.
        // Sensible fallback when the region fetch is skipped entirely: the plain
        // midpoint of each authored range, NOT run through the bias power. Bias
        // shapes a LIVE channel signal; with no signal at all, the raw midpoint is
        // the more predictable "what does this look like with variance off" than a
        // heavily-biased value parked near one end of the range would be. This also
        // happens to be exact rather than approximate: bRegionEnable is only false
        // when every range below is degenerate (min == max), in which case
        // VarianceRemap returns that same constant regardless of what t is fed in.
        float2 mDensity = float2(VarianceRemap(0.5f, WallDensityRange),
                                 VarianceRemap(0.5f, FilamentDensityRange));
        float mWallFalloff = VarianceRemap(0.5f, WallFalloffRange);
        float mInvWidth = 1.0f / max(VarianceRemap(0.5f, FeatureWidthRange), 1e-6f);
        float mVoidFloor = VarianceRemap(0.5f, VoidFloorRange);
        float mSpread = VarianceRemap(0.5f, VoidSizeSpreadRange);
        float mSkew = 1.0f;
        float mWarpLarge = VarianceRemap(0.5f, WarpAmountLargeRange);
        float mWarpSmall = VarianceRemap(0.5f, WarpAmountSmallRange);

        // THE LATTICE BLEND, and the plain midpoint is the right fallback for the same
        // reason it is everywhere else here: with no region signal, half way between
        // the two authored lattices is the predictable "what does this look like with
        // variance off," where a bias-shaped 0.5 would be a number nobody authored.
        float mLatticeBlend = 0.5f;


        // ==================================================================
        // FIELD A -- STRUCTURE, COARSE
        // ==================================================================
        // Everything that decides what SHAPE the web has here: which lattice, how much
        // the void sizes vary, how wide the features are, how hard the large octave
        // bends it. These change slowly, over provinces rather than patches, which is
        // why they get the coarser of the two scales.
        if (bRegionEnableA > 0.5f)
        {
            float4 a = RegionAxes(InTex, InSampler, cx, cy, cz, frac, RegionScaleA,
                                  float3(UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_U,
                                         UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_V,
                                         UNIVERSE_REGION_A_OFFSET_W));

            // a.x -> WHICH LATTICE. The largest structural switch in the layer, and it
            // gets a channel to itself. It used to share with void spread, which meant
            // every coarse-lattice region also ran at maximum spread -- large uniform
            // voids and small varied ones were both unreachable, and that is half the
            // structure space gone on one correlation.
            //
            // A raw VarianceT rather than a VarianceRemap: the blend IS the interpolant,
            // so there are no two extremes to lerp between -- the extremes are the two
            // lattices themselves.
            mLatticeBlend = VarianceT(a.x, CellSizeBias);

            // a.y -> VOID SIZE, both the spread and the skew that shapes its
            // distribution. These two stay together deliberately: skew decides how the
            // offsets are distributed and spread decides how far they reach, so they are
            // one setting rather than two that happen to correlate.
            float voidT = VarianceT(a.y, VoidSizeSpreadRange.z);
            mSpread = VarianceRemap(voidT, VoidSizeSpreadRange);

            // Off the RAW channel, not off spread's bias-shaped interpolant. Skew has no
            // range of its own to carry a bias in -- its value rides
            // VoidSizeSpreadRange.w -- so the choice is between chaining it to spread's
            // response curve or giving it the plain linear one. The plain one is looser:
            // chained, skew was an affine function of spread and could not have a
            // different shape at all.
            mSkew = 1.0f + (a.y * 2.0f - 1.0f) * 0.5f;

            // a.z -> FEATURE WIDTH, which is the single lambda for walls and filaments
            // alike since the measure collapsed to one weight set. It used to ride
            // FilamentDensity's channel, which was an accident of the name it had then
            // rather than a decision: a global structural parameter should not be a
            // function of one feature's brightness.
            float featureWidthT = VarianceT(a.z, FeatureWidthRange.z);
            mInvWidth = 1.0f / max(VarianceRemap(featureWidthT, FeatureWidthRange), 1e-6f);

            // a.w -> THE LARGE WARP OCTAVE. The two
            // octaves are on different fields now, which is the whole point: they do
            // different jobs -- one bends the structural distribution, the other breaks
            // up local form -- and on one channel "this region is macro-warped, that one
            // is micro-warped" was unreachable however the biases were authored.
            mWarpLarge = VarianceRemap(VarianceT(a.w, WarpAmountLargeRange.z),
                                       WarpAmountLargeRange);
        }

        // ==================================================================
        // FIELD B -- APPEARANCE, FINER
        // ==================================================================
        // Everything that decides how the web is PAINTED once its shape is settled, plus
        // the small octave's grain. These are free to change faster, and their boundaries
        // falling in different places from A's is what makes a province read as one place
        // with internal variety rather than thirteen properties all switching at once.
        if (bRegionEnableB > 0.5f)
        {
            float4 b = RegionAxes(InTex, InSampler, cx, cy, cz, frac, RegionScaleB,
                                  float3(UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_U,
                                         UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_V,
                                         UNIVERSE_REGION_B_OFFSET_W));

            // b.x -> WALL BRIGHTNESS, b.y -> WALL SHARPNESS. Separated because they are
            // independent things to want: a region of bright diffuse sheets and a region
            // of dim crisp ones are both reachable now, where on one channel only two of
            // those four corners were.
            mDensity.x = VarianceRemap(VarianceT(b.x, WallDensityRange.z),
                                       WallDensityRange);
            mWallFalloff = VarianceRemap(VarianceT(b.y, WallFalloffRange.z),
                                         WallFalloffRange);

            // b.z -> FILAMENT BRIGHTNESS, and the ambient floor with it. Kept off the
            // wall's channel, which is the correlation this layer most wants to avoid:
            // independent wall and filament densities are what give sheet-dominated and
            // strand-dominated regions, the most characterful variation available here.
            // The floor rides along because how much haze a region carries is a
            // reasonable thing to have track how much structure it has.
            float filamentT = VarianceT(b.z, FilamentDensityRange.z);
            mDensity.y = VarianceRemap(filamentT, FilamentDensityRange);
            mVoidFloor = VarianceRemap(VarianceT(b.z, VoidFloorRange.z), VoidFloorRange);

            // b.w -> THE SMALL WARP OCTAVE. See a.w.
            mWarpSmall = VarianceRemap(VarianceT(b.w, WarpAmountSmallRange.z),
                                       WarpAmountSmallRange);
        }

#if UNIVERSE_COST_PROBE < 2
        if (bWarpEnable > 0.5f)
        {
            // CELL-RELATIVE base coordinate. The integer cell is MASKED and only then
            // converted, so nothing ever holds 1e11 in a float and this is exact at
            // any magnitude the traversal reaches. Both scales are pre-quantized so
            // the masked wrap is congruent modulo one and the sampler's repeat
            // addressing closes it seamlessly.
            float bx = float(cx & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + frac.x;
            float by = float(cy & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + frac.y;
            float bz = float(cz & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + frac.z;

            // Both addresses computed before either fetch, so the two loads issue
            // together rather than one waiting on the other. This is the whole reason
            // the small octave is sampled at the unwarped coordinate.
            // THE SCALES ARE UNIFORM ACROSS THE DRAW and are not on the variance path
            // -- the reasoning, and the measurement from when they briefly were, is at
            // THE FETCH SCALES ARE FIXED near the top of this file. Both are unpacked
            // from their octave's amount range .w and quantized once.
            float3 uvL = float3(bx * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_X,
                                by * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Y,
                                bz * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Z);

            float3 uvS = float3(bx * WarpScaleSmall + UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_X,
                                by * WarpScaleSmall + UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_Y,
                                bz * WarpScaleSmall + UNIVERSE_WARP_SMALL_OFFSET_Z);

            // SampleLevel, not Sample. Inside a dynamic loop the derivatives that
            // drive mip selection are undefined, and the galaxy core makes the same
            // call for the same reason.
            // SAME TEXTURE now, but still two independent fetches: neither address
            // depends on the other's result, so they still issue together. What used
            // to make them independent -- different assets -- is gone; issuing
            // together never depended on that, only on the addresses being computed
            // first, which is still true.
            float4 vL = InTex.SampleLevel(InSampler, uvL, 0.0f);
            float4 vS = InTex.SampleLevel(InSampler, uvS, 0.0f);

            // Per-axis weighted decode now, instead of a bare UNORM->signed conversion
            // -- WarpLargeWeights/WarpSmallWeights let each displacement axis be
            // gained, zeroed, or inverted independently. See WeightedVector3.
            float3 dL = WeightedVector3(vL, WarpLargeWeights);
            float3 dS = WeightedVector3(vS, WarpSmallWeights);

            // mWarpLarge/mWarpSmall are the FULLY RESOLVED amounts now, already
            // picked off WarpAmountLargeRange/WarpAmountSmallRange above -- there is
            // no more struct-field amount to multiply by. Setting a range's min and
            // max equal reproduces the old fixed-amount behaviour exactly; setting
            // RegionWarp*Variance-equivalent range spread to zero-width on the large
            // octave reproduces the old "large octave never regionally moves" safety
            // margin against the fold ceiling, same as before, just expressed as a
            // degenerate range instead of a zeroed amount pin.
            float ampL = mWarpLarge;
            float ampS = mWarpSmall;

            // Applied directly, with no centring. On a signed [-1,1] field the peak
            // displacement IS the amplitude -- where the earlier [0,1] form built
            // (v - 0.5) * amp and peaked at half of it. So an amplitude carried over
            // from that form must be HALVED. Shear is unaffected either way: a
            // constant offset does not change a gradient, so the fold ceiling below
            // is written the same in both conventions.
            //
            // Kept as warpDelta rather than folded straight into frac, because the
            // coarse lattice re-derives its own displacement from the same raw value
            // below on different terms.
            warpDelta = float3(dL.x * ampL + dS.x * ampS,
                               dL.y * ampL + dS.y * ampS,
                               dL.z * ampL + dS.z * ampS);

            float wx = frac.x + warpDelta.x;
            float wy = frac.y + warpDelta.y;
            float wz = frac.z + warpDelta.z;

            // ==================================================================
            // THE TWO OCTAVES REACH THE COARSE LATTICE ON OPPOSITE TERMS
            // ==================================================================
            // Each octave has two things that could follow the grid -- how far it
            // displaces and how far apart its features are -- and the answer is
            // different for each octave because the two are doing different jobs.
            // Settled by looking at all four combinations on screen; the reasoning below
            // is what the result turned out to mean rather than what predicted it.
            //
            //                        WAVELENGTH        AMPLITUDE
            //   large octave         scales            WarpLargeWeights.w
            //   small octave         fixed             scales
            //
            // THE LARGE OCTAVE SHAPES THE STRUCTURAL DISTRIBUTION. What it is for is the
            // overarching bend of the lattice, so the bend has to happen over a distance
            // comparable to the cells being bent -- a wavelength pinned to fine cells is
            // high-frequency chatter to a lattice three times coarser, and it stops
            // describing the shape of anything. So its UV comes from the COARSE cell
            // index and its pattern is ratio times larger in world terms. Its AMPLITUDE
            // does not follow: how far the universe is displaced at large scale is a
            // property of the universe, not of which lattice happens to be reading it,
            // and scaling it up as well made the coarse regions bend visibly harder than
            // the fine ones for no reason anyone could point at.
            //
            // THE SMALL OCTAVE BREAKS THE FORM UP ORGANICALLY. What it is for is
            // roughening whatever boundary it lands on, so it has to be a fraction of
            // THAT boundary's cell -- an amplitude pinned to an absolute size does
            // nothing visible once the cells are several times larger than it, and the
            // coarse web arrives with its straight Voronoi edges essentially intact. So
            // its amplitude is read as coarse cells. Its WAVELENGTH does not follow:
            // scaling it turns grain into a second large octave, and what that does to
            // the coarse lattice is not "more warp" but a breaking-up of the local form,
            // the displacement growing while the wavelength stays put.
            //
            // FOLD BUDGET. Shear is amplitude * scale * G in the lattice's own units.
            // The large octave now has both halves matched to the lattice, so it pays
            // ratio times LESS on the coarse lattice than on the fine one. The small
            // octave holds amplitude against a fixed wavelength, so it pays ratio times
            // more. The ceiling the coarse lattice has to respect is
            //
            //     (AmountLarge*ScaleLarge/ratio + AmountSmall*ScaleSmall*ratio) * G <= 1
            //
            // and the small octave is what binds. Past it the coarse web tears rather
            // than bends, and it tears ONLY in the regions the blend has handed to it --
            // a failure that appears in some places and not others, which is a
            // particularly easy one to misread as something else.
            //
            // THE COST IS ONE TEXTURE READ and one thing given up: the two lattices no
            // longer share a single displacement field for the large octave, since they
            // read the same texture at two different scales. Through the crossover band
            // the fine and coarse webs bend somewhat independently rather than together.
            float3 coarseDelta = warpDelta;

            if (bLatticeLarge > 0.5f)
            {
                // Same masked cell-relative construction as above, on the COARSE index.
                // The wrap closes for the same reason: WarpScaleLarge is quantized to
                // 1/UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP, so WRAP * scale is a whole number whichever
                // lattice's index is being masked.
                float lbx = float(lx & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + lfrac.x;
                float lby = float(ly & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + lfrac.y;
                float lbz = float(lz & UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP_MASK) + lfrac.z;

                float3 uvLC = float3(
                    lbx * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_X,
                    lby * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Y,
                    lbz * WarpScaleLarge + UNIVERSE_WARP_LARGE_OFFSET_Z);

                float4 vLC = InTex.SampleLevel(InSampler, uvLC, 0.0f);
                float3 dLC = WeightedVector3(vLC, WarpLargeWeights);

                // HOW MUCH OF THE RATIO THE AMPLITUDE FOLLOWS, from
                // WarpLargeWeights.w, and this is a dial rather than a decision because
                // the two ends are both defensible and both were tried:
                //
                //   0  the amplitude does not follow at all. The coarse tier gets the
                //      same PHYSICAL displacement, which is 1/ratio of a coarse cell
                //      against a full amplitude of a fine one, so the coarse web bends
                //      proportionally less. Scaling the wavelength alone leaves this,
                //      and the wavelength scaling is close to invisible on its own --
                //      the pattern grows with the cells and reads as self-similar --
                //      so what shows is the tiers bending by different amounts.
                //   1  the amplitude follows completely. The coarse tier sees the
                //      identical warp geometry at its own scale, wavelength and
                //      displacement both, which is genuine self-similarity and bends
                //      the coarse web ratio times further in world terms.
                //
                // Between them the coarse tier bends less than the fine one but more
                // than an absolute displacement would give, which is where this is
                // likely to want to sit -- a large void being stiffer than a small one
                // is physically reasonable, being ratio times stiffer is a big claim.
                //
                // WarpLargeWeights.w is the host because it was the only unused handle
                // that belongs to this exact octave: the weights vector gains three
                // displacement axes and there is no fourth. WarpSmallWeights.w is still
                // free and is where the same dial would go if the small octave ever
                // wants one -- it is currently pinned at full follow, which is the
                // "scales its amount" row of the table above.
                //
                // FOLD BUDGET IS BOUNDED EITHER WAY. Wavelength and amplitude are both
                // matched to the coarse lattice at follow 1, so the shear there is
                // exactly the fine tier's; at follow 0 it is the fine tier's divided by
                // the ratio. This octave cannot exceed the ceiling the fine lattice
                // already imposes, whatever the dial is set to.
                float ampLC = ampL * lerp(InvLatticeRatio, 1.0f,
                                          saturate(WarpLargeWeights.w));

                coarseDelta = float3(dLC.x * ampLC + dS.x * ampS,
                                     dLC.y * ampLC + dS.y * ampS,
                                     dLC.z * ampLC + dS.z * ampS);
            }

            float lwx = lfrac.x + coarseDelta.x;
            float lwy = lfrac.y + coarseDelta.y;
            float lwz = lfrac.z + coarseDelta.z;

            float lix = floor(lwx);
            float liy = floor(lwy);
            float liz = floor(lwz);

            lx += (int) lix;
            ly += (int) liy;
            lz += (int) liz;
            lfrac = float3(lwx - lix, lwy - liy, lwz - liz);

            // Re-normalizes the split, so the displaced sample is still an exact
            // integer cell plus a fraction in [0,1). Displacing a reconstructed
            // float3 would work for a few thousand cells and then quietly stop
            // resolving.
            float ix = floor(wx);
            float iy = floor(wy);
            float iz = floor(wz);

            cx += (int) ix;
            cy += (int) iy;
            cz += (int) iz;
            frac = float3(wx - ix, wy - iy, wz - iz);
        }
#endif

        // ==================================================================
        // THE DIAGRAM
        // ==================================================================
        // WIDTH IS CONSUMED HERE, not in DensityFromWeb. It sets how fast a candidate's
        // weight falls off with distance, which is upstream of the measure rather than
        // downstream of it. Wall falloff and the floor go the other way, into the
        // composition, since neither touches where the nodes are.
        //
        // LAMBDA, PIVOT AND SUPPRESSION ARE RESOLVED HERE rather than inside the walk
        // because BOTH LATTICES MUST SHARE THEM. Their weights land in one pair of
        // accumulators, so a per-lattice lambda or pivot would make the two sets
        // incomparable and the participant count meaningless. See TWO LATTICES, ONE
        // DIAGRAM.
        // A ratio of one means the two lattices ARE the same lattice: identical cell
        // indices, identical hashes, identical nodes. Pinned to the fine lattice alone,
        // which is exactly the single-lattice field this layer had before, at no extra
        // cost.
        float tLattice = (bLatticeLarge > 0.5f) ? mLatticeBlend : 0.0f;

        // The coarse lattice's squared distances are ratio^2 times the fine lattice's
        // for the same physical separation. This is the conversion that puts both node
        // sets in one metric, and it multiplies the spread with them so void-size
        // variation stays the same FRACTION of a cell on both.
        float ratioSq = LatticeRatio * LatticeRatio;

        // SUPPRESSION, added to every FINE candidate's power distance and to none of the
        // coarse ones. The mean power distance to a nearest node is
        // UNIVERSE_WEIGHT_PIVOT in a lattice's own units, so in shared units the gap
        // between the two lattices averages pivot * (ratio^2 - 1); running the
        // suppression to twice that across the blend puts the crossover in the middle
        // of the range instead of jammed against one end.
        float latticeGap = UNIVERSE_WEIGHT_PIVOT * (ratioSq - 1.0f);

        // THE RAMP IS THE ODDS RATIO, t/(1-t), and a linear one is wrong in a way that
        // is invisible until you look for it. Linear reaches twice the gap at the end of
        // the blend, which beats the coarse lattice's TYPICAL node and not its far ones
        // -- so inside the coarse lattice's larger voids, where its nearest node is
        // unusually distant, the still-present fine lattice wins and fills the void in
        // with fine structure. Measured at ratio 3: 5.5% of samples differed by more
        // than half the feature amplitude between a blend of 0.993 and one of 1.0, and
        // the mean density sat three times too high across the whole top of the range.
        // Big voids that are not empty are the one thing this feature exists to avoid.
        //
        // t/(1-t) is zero at zero, exactly the gap at the midpoint -- so the crossover
        // still lands where the pivot expects it -- and diverges at one, which
        // extinguishes the fine lattice outright rather than merely outvoting it. The
        // guard on the denominator caps it at ten thousand times the gap, which is
        // already far past the point every fine weight has underflowed to zero.
        float odds = tLattice / max(1.0f - tLattice, 1e-4f);
        float suppress = latticeGap * odds;

        // How far along the fine-to-coarse crossover this sample sits, 1 to ratio^2,
        // saturating at the midpoint because past there the coarse lattice is what is
        // being measured and its distances do not move any further. It tracks the
        // SUPPRESSED fine lattice below the midpoint and the coarse lattice above it,
        // which is exactly where the dominant candidate is in each case.
        float effRatio = 1.0f + (ratioSq - 1.0f) * min(odds, 1.0f);

        // LAMBDA FOLLOWS THE RAMP, and this is what makes the two ends of the blend
        // exact rather than merely similar. Apparent wall thickness is 1/(4*lambda*d)
        // with d in shared units, and the coarse lattice's separations are `ratio` times
        // larger there, so a fixed lambda gives its sheets the same ABSOLUTE thickness
        // as the fine lattice's -- which is a ratio^2 smaller fraction of its own much
        // bigger cells. At ratio 12 that is a web of hairlines around enormous voids.
        //
        // Dividing by effRatio cancels it exactly. It is one scalar shared by BOTH
        // lattices at a given sample, so nothing about comparability changes -- and at
        // either end of the blend the exponent reduces to lambda * (pivot - u) in that
        // lattice's OWN cell units, which is to say the field becomes precisely the
        // single-lattice field at that cell size. Verified: pinning the blend to either
        // end reproduces the corresponding single-lattice configuration's statistics.
        //
        // IT USES ITS OWN RAMP, GEOMETRIC, and not the pivot's. The pivot's ramp
        // saturates at the middle of the blend because the dominant candidate's DISTANCE
        // stops moving there -- both lattices sit at the same u by construction, which is
        // what a crossover is. Feature SCALE does not stop there: the two lattices still
        // have separations a full ratio^2 apart, and a shared lambda has to sit between
        // them rather than jumping to one end. Reusing the pivot's ramp gives the coarse
        // lattice's width to the fine lattice's structure through the whole second half
        // of the blend, and the field turns to mush -- measured, at ratio 12, as a mean
        // density of 1.89 against 0.06 at either end.
        //
        // Geometric rather than linear because a scale interpolates that way: halfway
        // between a lattice and one three times coarser is a factor of three, not two.
        // exp2(log2(ratio^2) * t) is one exp2 against a precomputed constant, and it is
        // exactly 1 and exactly ratio^2 at the two ends, so both limits stay exact.
        //
        // The clamp is applied before the division, so it stays a bound on the steepest
        // lambda the walk can ever see.
        // The exponent is a SMOOTHSTEP CENTRED ON THE CROSSOVER, not t itself, because
        // which lattice is dominant is a near-step function of t and not a gradual
        // slide: the fine and coarse dominant weights differ by lambda * latticeGap *
        // (1 - odds) in the exponent, and lambda * latticeGap runs to about 200, so the
        // handover happens within a couple of percent of the midpoint. Ramping lambda
        // linearly in t across the whole range instead leaves the coarse lattice being
        // measured with a lambda still several times too steep through the upper half
        // of the blend -- measured at ratio 4 as a mean density of 0.020 at t = 0.73
        // against 0.059 at both ends, a dip to a third of the field it sits between.
        //
        // The band is +-0.15 in t, which is wide enough to stay smooth and narrow
        // enough to have handed over before the coarse lattice is on its own. A region
        // boundary is a fairly sharp thing here rather than a long morph, which is what
        // the structure being described actually looks like: a patch of small voids, a
        // patch of large ones, and an edge between them.
        float lamT = saturate((tLattice - 0.35f) * (1.0f / 0.3f));
        lamT = lamT * lamT * (3.0f - 2.0f * lamT);
        float lamRatio = exp2(LatticeLogRatioSq * lamT);
        float lam = min(UNIVERSE_WIDTH_TO_LAMBDA * mInvWidth, UNIVERSE_LAMBDA_MAX)
                  / lamRatio;

        // THE PIVOT TRACKS THE CROSSOVER. It only recentres the exponent -- the measure
        // is invariant under a common scaling of the weights -- but it has to land near
        // whatever the DOMINANT candidate's u actually is, and that is no longer a
        // constant: it walks from the fine lattice's typical distance up to the coarse
        // lattice's as the suppression takes hold, then stops, because past the
        // crossover the coarse lattice is what is being measured and it does not move.
        // Leaving the pivot at the fine lattice's value underflows every weight to zero
        // at ratio 3 and returns pure void.
        // Lambda's ramp is in t rather than in the odds, deliberately: the odds diverge
        // and would take lambda to zero.
        float uTypical = UNIVERSE_WEIGHT_PIVOT * effRatio;

        // The spread term tracks the pivot down as the power-diagram offset pulls u
        // negative. Nothing bounds the pivot from above any more: the exponent's
        // overflow guard moved into the walk, where it costs one min per candidate and
        // does not distort the one number that has to stay where the dominant candidate
        // is. See the clamp in LatticeWeightSums.
        // THE SPREAD TERM SCALES TOO, and with LAMBDA'S ramp rather than the pivot's.
        // The coarse lattice's offsets are multiplied by ratio^2 along with its
        // distances, so at ratio 3 an authored spread of 0.1 moves a coarse candidate's
        // u by up to 0.9 -- seventy-two in the exponent at a typical lambda -- and a
        // pivot subtracting the unscaled spread leaves the whole coarse lattice riding
        // the overflow clamp instead of resolving. But it is the DOMINANT lattice's
        // offsets that need centring, and which lattice that is is lambda's question,
        // not the pivot's: using effRatio here subtracts a coarse-sized allowance while
        // the fine lattice is still in charge, which pushes the far candidates into
        // underflow early and costs about a sixth of the density through the lower half
        // of the blend.
        float pivot = uTypical - 0.5f * mSpread * lamRatio;

        // BOTH WALKS FEED ONE PAIR OF SUMS, and ONLY THE FINE ONE CAN BE SKIPPED. The
        // asymmetry is not an oversight, it is the shape of the mechanism:
        //
        //   The suppression is an additive offset on EVERY fine candidate, so it puts a
        //   FLOOR under their power distance. u is at least suppress - spread whatever
        //   the hash produces, which bounds the largest weight the fine lattice can
        //   contribute, and past some suppression that bound is below float resolution.
        //   A real bound, so a sound skip.
        //
        //   The coarse lattice has no such offset. It is only outranged ON AVERAGE, and
        //   its power distances are ratio^2 wider in shared units, so its lower tail
        //   reaches far below its mean -- a coarse node with a large radius offset sits
        //   at -spread * ratio^2, which beats every fine candidate no matter how the
        //   blend is set. There is no threshold that makes the coarse walk skippable.
        //
        // A typical-case margin was tried for both sides and is WRONG for the coarse one,
        // measured: skipping the coarse walk when the fine lattice led by 40 powers of
        // two on average changed the mean density by 17% at a blend of zero and by 46% at
        // 0.25, with individual samples moving by the full feature amplitude. The mean
        // gap there is 198 powers of two. Distributions this skewed are not summarised by
        // their means.
        //
        // THAT INTRUSION IS LOAD-BEARING, not a defect to fix. Occasional coarse nodes
        // punching through a fine region are what put the odd large void among small
        // ones, and it is live across the whole blend range in the field as it stands.
        //
        // THE RATIO-1 CASE IS GATED SEPARATELY: with one lattice the coarse walk runs
        // against a node set identical to the fine one, at double cost, for a field it
        // cannot change.
        float fineCeiling = lam * (pivot + mSpread - suppress);

        float sumW = 0.0f;
        float sumSqW = 0.0f;

        if (fineCeiling > -UNIVERSE_LATTICE_SKIP)
        {
            float2 fine = LatticeWeightSums(cx, cy, cz, frac, lam, pivot,
                                            mSpread, mSkew, 1.0f, suppress);
            sumW += fine.x;
            sumSqW += fine.y;
        }

        if (bLatticeLarge > 0.5f)
        {
            // Same spread, skew and width NUMBERS, converted into shared units by
            // ratioSq rather than re-authored. That is what makes the two lattices one
            // field at two scales instead of two unrelated fields: a void's size
            // variation is the same fraction of its own cell on both.
            float2 coarse = LatticeWeightSums(lx, ly, lz, lfrac, lam, pivot,
                                              mSpread, mSkew, ratioSq, 0.0f);
            sumW += coarse.x;
            sumSqW += coarse.y;
        }

        // Division guard. Every weight is strictly positive so the denominator cannot be
        // zero mathematically, but it is the square of an already-small number and the
        // pivot only bounds how small. On underflow the measure returns zero, which the
        // saturates in DensityFromWeb read as void -- a collapse to the floor rather
        // than a NaN spreading through the march.
        float measure = (sumW * sumW) / max(sumSqW, 1e-30f);

        float D = DensityFromWeb(measure, mDensity, mWallFalloff, mVoidFloor);

        return D;
    }

    float SampleAtPosition(Texture3D InTex, SamplerState InSampler, float3 InNormPos)
    {
        // The fractional offset is added BEFORE the floor, so its carry into the
        // integer cell falls out of the same floor that decomposes the position.
        float3 cellPos = InNormPos * InvCellSizeSmall + OffsetFrac;

        // floor, not truncation: truncation folds the cell boundary at zero and
        // mirrors the web across every axis through the origin.
        float fx = floor(cellPos.x);
        float fy = floor(cellPos.y);
        float fz = floor(cellPos.z);

        float3 frac = float3(cellPos.x - fx, cellPos.y - fy, cellPos.z - fz);

        int cx = (int) fx + OffsetCellX;
        int cy = (int) fy + OffsetCellY;
        int cz = (int) fz + OffsetCellZ;

        // THE LARGE LATTICE'S SPLIT, and the whole reason the ratio is a whole number.
        // The position in large cells is (k + cellPos) / ratio, and k reaches 1e11, so
        // the division cannot touch it as a float. Writing k as ratio*kq + kr with kq
        // and kr whole gives
        //
        //     (k + cellPos) / ratio  =  kq + (kr + cellPos) / ratio
        //
        // where kq is the large-cell index outright and everything divided is small.
        // Integer division is exact at any magnitude; a float multiply by 1/ratio is not,
        // and its error changes as k crosses a cell plane, which would shift the large
        // lattice bodily and discontinuously as the player moves.
        //
        // kq and kr ARE PRECOMPUTED. They depend only on the field offset and the ratio,
        // both constant for the draw, so what is left here per sample is three multiplies
        // and a floor -- see the offset fields in the struct.
        float3 largePos = float3(
            (LargeOffsetRemX + cellPos.x) * InvLatticeRatio,
            (LargeOffsetRemY + cellPos.y) * InvLatticeRatio,
            (LargeOffsetRemZ + cellPos.z) * InvLatticeRatio);

        float lfx = floor(largePos.x);
        float lfy = floor(largePos.y);
        float lfz = floor(largePos.z);

        float3 lfrac = float3(largePos.x - lfx, largePos.y - lfy, largePos.z - lfz);

        int lx = (int) lfx + LargeOffsetCellX;
        int ly = (int) lfy + LargeOffsetCellY;
        int lz = (int) lfz + LargeOffsetCellZ;

        return SampleCell(InTex, InSampler,
                          cx, cy, cz, frac,
                          lx, ly, lz, lfrac);
    }

};

// Snaps a texture repeats-per-cell to a multiple of 1/UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP, and this is
// correctness rather than taste. The UV wraps every UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP cells by masking
// the cell index; the two sides of that wrap are the same point in the texture only if
// WRAP * scale is a whole number, and otherwise there is a hard seam every 4096 cells
// that no amount of amplitude tuning will hide. The quantum is 1/4096 of a repeat per
// cell, far below any tuning granularity that matters.
//
// floor(x + 0.5) rather than round(): HLSL rounds halves away from zero and the C++
// shim's round() defers to nearbyint, which rounds halves to even. A scale landing
// exactly on a half would then quantize differently on the two sides, which is exactly
// the class of silent divergence the shared file exists to prevent.
//
// FILE SCOPE, not a member of UniverseDensityParams, because its only caller is the
// function that CONSTRUCTS one -- calling through a half-built P would compile in both
// languages and read like a mistake. All four scales go through it.
float QuantizeScale(float InScale)
{
    return floor(max(InScale, 0.0f) * UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP + 0.5f) / UNIVERSE_WARP_WRAP;
}

// Floor division by a POSITIVE divisor. Both languages truncate toward zero, which
// splits the number line at the origin the same way truncation instead of floor would
// mirror the web through it -- and for the same reason it is wrong here. The remainder
// has to stay in [0, divisor) on both sides of zero or the large lattice reflects across
// the origin.
//
// FILE SCOPE for the same reason QuantizeScale is: its only caller is now the constructor
// below. It used to run three times PER SAMPLE inside SampleAtPosition, which is where
// this hoist came from -- integer division by a runtime divisor is not one instruction on
// a GPU, it expands to a reciprocal sequence, so three of them on every march step was
// costing more than the whole rest of the coordinate split put together. Every input it
// takes is constant for the draw.
int FloorDiv(int InValue, int InDivisor)
{
    int q = InValue / InDivisor;
    return (InValue < 0 && q * InDivisor != InValue) ? q - 1 : q;
}

// ==========================================================================
// DERIVATION
// The single place inversion and derived quantities are computed. Both the
// shader and C++ call this, which is what keeps their fields identical rather
// than merely intended to match.
//
// UNPACKED, one scalar per term, EXCEPT the eight fields the variance redesign
// packed into (min, max, bias, free). Those are no longer "a default plus a
// separate region amount" -- there is no default any more, just two authored
// extremes and a bias curve for where between them a region falls:
//
//   WallDensityRange        WallFalloffRange
//   FilamentDensityRange    FeatureWidthRange
//   VoidFloorRange          VoidSizeSpreadRange
//   WarpAmountLargeRange    WarpAmountSmallRange
//
// WHICH FIELD AND CHANNEL EACH ONE READS is in the struct, above RegionScaleA. Moving a
// range between channels is one letter in SampleCell plus one line in the matching
// bRegionEnable test, and the second is the one that gets forgotten.
//
// This layer has fourteen parameters, twelve of which are float4: the eight ranges
// above, CellSizeRange, RegionScales, and one weight vector per warp octave's
// displacement axes. It was twenty-three before the cluster/gas stage came out, which
// is most of what that stage cost to author.
//
// CellSizeRange IS PACKED BUT IS NOT A RANGE in the sense the others are. The rest
// name two extremes of one quantity and resolve to a value between them; this one
// names two LATTICES and resolves which of them a sample reads. Its .z is still a
// bias and behaves the same way, but there is nothing to lerp, so it never passes
// through VarianceRemap and it is absent from the packing table above.
//
// FOUR VECTORS CARRY A PASSENGER IN .w, which was documented as a free per-field
// handle from the day the packing convention landed:
//
//   VoidSizeSpreadRange.w    VoidSizeSkew
//   WarpAmountLargeRange.w   WarpScaleLarge
//   WarpAmountSmallRange.w   WarpScaleSmall
//   WarpLargeWeights.w       how much of the lattice ratio the large octave's
//                            amplitude follows on the coarse tier
//
// The test each of these passes, and the one to apply to the next candidate: the
// passenger belongs to the SAME FIELD as its host, so a pin carrying both is one
// setting rather than two unrelated ones sharing a wire. Skew already rode spread's
// own interpolant. Each warp scale multiplies its octave's amount in the shear
// product, so neither number means anything without the other. And the large octave's
// lattice-follow sits in the weights vector that already gains that same octave's three
// displacement axes, where .w had nothing to steer.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT UNRANGED is not an accident of which pass reached which pin. CellSize
// cannot be, and that is structural rather than a matter of degree -- see the note at
// RegionAxes. Neither region scale must be, or a field decides how big its own regions
// are -- at a position it can only know by having already fetched itself. Both warp
// scales were ranged and were rolled back after looking at the result -- see THE FETCH
// SCALES ARE FIXED. VoidSizeSkew and the enables are riders and switches rather than
// quantities.
//
// NO RANGE CLAMPING. Every parameter passes through at whatever value it is
// given, so the effect of pushing one past its sensible range is visible rather
// than silently absorbed. What remains are division guards and one loop bound,
// which are correctness rather than taste, and each is marked. The ranges that
// used to be clamped are documented at their parameter instead -- the numbers
// are worth keeping even though the enforcement is not:
//
//   *Range.w        A free per-field handle. CLAIMED on VoidSizeSpread, both
//                   WarpAmounts and WarpLargeWeights, and unclaimed on the rest; the
//                   list and the test a new claim should pass are in the DERIVATION
//                   block above. A .w is deliberately NOT part of the
//                   min/max degeneracy test that gates the region fetch, so setting one
//                   never makes a constant range look variable.
//   *Range.z (bias) <= 0 is guarded by PowSafe against a zero-based pow, same as
//                   every other exponent in this file: it returns 0 rather than
//                   blowing up, which reads as "always at max" once w=0's own
//                   0-power-of-0 case is accounted for -- worth an eye if a bias of
//                   exactly 0 is ever authored on purpose.
//   *Range.x/.y     min > max is VALID and simply runs the interpolation in
//                   reverse; there is no requirement that x be the smaller value.
//   VoidSizeSpreadRange  SQUARED cell units now, not cells, and values carried over
//                   from the additive form do not mean what they used to. It is
//                   subtracted from the squared distance, so a node out-reaches an
//                   unoffset neighbour by `spread` in squared distance -- which at a
//                   typical half-cell separation is about `spread` in linear distance
//                   too, but shrinks as the separation grows. The shipped 0.80 was
//                   authored against a form with no bound at all and is very likely
//                   too large here; start an order lower and come up.
//   VoidSizeSpreadRange.y  still the thing that erodes the 27-cell search, but it now
//                   does so by a stated amount rather than an unbounded one: the
//                   window is sound while spread stays well under 1.0 minus the
//                   dominant candidate's own power distance, and the weight ratio at
//                   a cell-plane transition is 2^(-lambda*(1.0 - spread - u1)). See
//                   the TRUNCATION paragraph at WeightedNodeMeasure, and note that
//                   the bound degrades with WIDE features as well as large spread.
//   WarpAmount*Range peak displacement in cells, and NOT the convention the retired
//                   lattice path used: that one centred and doubled, so any value
//                   carried over from it must be doubled to displace the same
//                   distance.
//   WarpAmount*Range.w  texture repeats per cell, fixed across the draw. What curves
//                   the web is SHEAR, which is amount * scale * G summed over both
//                   octaves, so neither half of one of these pins means anything alone
//                   and the fold ceiling
//                       AmountLarge*ScaleLarge + AmountSmall*ScaleSmall <= 1/G
//                   which the COARSE lattice rewrites: the small octave's term is
//                   multiplied by the cell size ratio and the large octave's is divided
//                   by it, because the small octave scales its amplitude against a fixed
//                   wavelength and the large octave does the reverse. The small octave is
//                   what binds there -- see the warp block in SampleCell
//                   couples all four numbers. Check it against each range's .y (max),
//                   since that is the amount a region can actually resolve to; the
//                   scales are fixed and enter as themselves. Raising either octave
//                   eats the other's headroom. Small scale with large amplitude
//                   translates whole regions rigidly and the straight Voronoi edges
//                   arrive straight; large scale with small amplitude is grain rather
//                   than curvature.
//   WarpTexGradient a property of the ASSET, and the one pin here that is measured
//                   rather than chosen. Wrong in both directions: understate it and
//                   the march skips further than it may and steps over features while
//                   the mean error stays negligible; overstate it and the skip
//                   collapses and the march does work it did not need to. A 64^3
//                   three-octave smooth volume measures 14.09. If the amplitudes look
//                   right but the predicted shear is far above one, this pin is what
//                   disagrees -- the ratio between where the web ACTUALLY tears and
//                   1/(scale * G) is the factor to correct it by. Becomes 1 and then
//                   disappears once volumes are gradient-normalized at bake time.
//   VoidFloorRange  below zero makes density negative, which the ray march's
//                   exp(-density) turns into unbounded gain rather than opacity. Both
//                   ends, now that it is ranged: a range spanning zero puts the
//                   negative end somewhere, and where is decided by a noise channel.
//   WallFalloffRange  exponent on saturate(N - 1). Below zero is a division-by-zero
//                   shape that PowSafe returns 0 for at the base epsilon and a
//                   blow-up just above it, so the void lights up rather than clearing;
//                   0 makes every non-zero base read as 1 and floods the field. 1 is
//                   the bare saturate, and up is the direction that clears voids.
// ==========================================================================
UniverseDensityParams MakeUniverseDensityParams(
    // --- WEB GEOMETRY ---
    // PACKED (small, large, bias, free). .y is quantized to a whole multiple of .x --
    // see TWO LATTICES, ONE BLEND, and the struct field, for why that is precision
    // rather than convenience. Setting them equal, or within half a small cell of each
    // other, gives one lattice and skips the second walk entirely.
    float4 InCellSizeRange,
    float InSeed,

    // --- FIELD OFFSET --- the player's position in the field, split so the integer part
    // carries magnitude and the fraction carries precision. Constant for the draw, which
    // is why it is resolved here rather than handed to every sample: the re-split into
    // large cells is integer division, and doing it per march step was the most expensive
    // thing in the coordinate path.
    float3 InOffsetCell,
    float3 InOffsetFrac,

    // --- WEB DENSITY, WIDTH AND FALLOFF --- what the march accumulates is density
    // times VOLUME FRACTION, and a wall covers an order of magnitude more volume than
    // a filament, so equal-looking densities make the field read as foam.
    // PACKED (min, max, bias, free): see VarianceRemap.
    float4 InWallDensityRange,
    float4 InWallFalloffRange, // exponent on the wall term; 1 is the bare saturate
    float4 InFilamentDensityRange,
    float4 InFeatureWidthRange, // weight-halving distance, cells; the ONE lambda
    float4 InVoidFloorRange, // ambient everywhere

    // --- ORGANICS ---
    float4 InVoidSizeSpreadRange, // .xyz offset per node, SQUARED cell units; .w SKEW
    float4 InWarpAmountLargeRange, // .xyz displacement in cells; .w repeats per cell
    float4 InWarpAmountSmallRange, // .xyz displacement in cells; .w repeats per cell
    float4 InWarpLargeWeights, // per-axis gain on the large octave's displacement
    float4 InWarpSmallWeights, // per-axis gain on the small octave's displacement
    // PACKED, and not a range: .x is the structure field's repeats per cell and .y the
    // appearance field's. Two numbers that belong to one system, in one pin, the same
    // way CellSizeRange carries two lattices. .zw unclaimed.
    float4 InRegionScales, // x structure 0.012939 (77 cells), y appearance 0.029053

    // --- BOUNDS --- set >= 1 to disable
    float InBoundsFadeStart)
{
    UniverseDensityParams P;

    // Division guard, not a range limit. A zero cell size is a division by zero, and
    // the resulting infinity propagates into every feature test at once.
    float cellSmall = max(InCellSizeRange.x, 1e-6f);
    P.InvCellSizeSmall = 1.0f / cellSmall;

    // THE RATIO IS ROUNDED TO A WHOLE NUMBER and floored at one. Rounded because the
    // offset re-split has to be integer division to stay exact at 1e11; floored at one
    // because a large cell smaller than the small one is not a second scale, it is the
    // same two lattices with their roles swapped and the blend running backwards.
    // Authoring .y below .x therefore collapses to a single lattice rather than doing
    // something surprising.
    float ratio = max(floor(max(InCellSizeRange.y, 0.0f) / cellSmall + 0.5f), 1.0f);
    P.LatticeRatio = ratio;
    P.LatticeRatioInt = (int) ratio;
    P.InvLatticeRatio = 1.0f / ratio;

    // log2 rather than a repeated multiply: the consumer wants exp2 of a fraction of
    // this, which is a scale interpolation, and doing it in the log domain is both the
    // correct interpolation and one instruction. Guarded only by the floor at 1 above,
    // which already keeps the argument at or above zero.
    P.LatticeLogRatioSq = 2.0f * log2(ratio);
    P.CellSizeBias = InCellSizeRange.z;
    P.bLatticeLarge = (ratio > 1.5f) ? 1.0f : 0.0f;

    P.SeedHash = (uint) ((int) InSeed) * 2654435761u;

    // THE OFFSET, SPLIT ONCE. kq is the offset expressed in whole large cells and the
    // remainder joins the fraction; see the derivation at SampleAtPosition for why this
    // has to be integer division rather than a float multiply by 1/ratio.
    P.OffsetFrac = InOffsetFrac;

    int kx = (int) InOffsetCell.x;
    int ky = (int) InOffsetCell.y;
    int kz = (int) InOffsetCell.z;
    P.OffsetCellX = kx;
    P.OffsetCellY = ky;
    P.OffsetCellZ = kz;

    int ratioInt = (int) ratio;
    int kqx = FloorDiv(kx, ratioInt);
    int kqy = FloorDiv(ky, ratioInt);
    int kqz = FloorDiv(kz, ratioInt);
    P.LargeOffsetCellX = kqx;
    P.LargeOffsetCellY = kqy;
    P.LargeOffsetCellZ = kqz;
    P.LargeOffsetRemX = float(kx - kqx * ratioInt);
    P.LargeOffsetRemY = float(ky - kqy * ratioInt);
    P.LargeOffsetRemZ = float(kz - kqz * ratioInt);

    P.WallDensityRange = InWallDensityRange;
    P.WallFalloffRange = InWallFalloffRange;
    P.FilamentDensityRange = InFilamentDensityRange;
    P.FeatureWidthRange = InFeatureWidthRange;
    P.VoidFloorRange = InVoidFloorRange;

    P.VoidSizeSpreadRange = InVoidSizeSpreadRange;
    // UNPACKED FROM .w, all three of them. A packed handle is only worth having if
    // the unpack is in one place; these are it.
    P.VoidSizeSkew = InVoidSizeSpreadRange.w;
    P.WarpAmountLargeRange = InWarpAmountLargeRange;
    P.WarpAmountSmallRange = InWarpAmountSmallRange;
    P.WarpScaleLarge = QuantizeScale(InWarpAmountLargeRange.w);
    P.WarpScaleSmall = QuantizeScale(InWarpAmountSmallRange.w);
    P.WarpLargeWeights = InWarpLargeWeights;
    P.WarpSmallWeights = InWarpSmallWeights;

    // ALL FOUR SCALES ARE QUANTIZED HERE, once, through the same helper. The region
    // fetch's own frequency is the one that could never have gone anywhere else: it is
    // what DEFINES where a region is, so putting it on the variance path would make the
    // definition self-referential -- the field deciding how big its own regions are, at
    // a position it can only know by having already fetched itself.
    //
    // KEEP IT OFF A SIMPLE RATIO WITH THE WARP SCALES. The obvious 1/32 = 0.03125
    // against a large octave at 0.05 is 8:5, so region structure and warp structure
    // re-align every 160 cells -- close enough to be reachable in one session. 0.0293
    // is about 34 cells and shares no small factor with either octave. That check is
    // again against two fixed numbers rather than against a resolved band -- and now
    // against each other as well, which is the third pair.
    P.RegionScaleA = QuantizeScale(InRegionScales.x);
    P.RegionScaleB = QuantizeScale(InRegionScales.y);

    // THE FOLD CEILING IS NO LONGER COMPUTED HERE. It used to fall out of a Lipschitz
    // bound on F2-F1 that the empty-space skip needed, and the skip is gone, so the two
    // gradient pins that fed it went with it -- they had no other consumer. The bound
    // itself is gone twice over now: F2-F1 does not exist any more either, and the
    // weighted measure is not a distance difference, so a bound on it would have to be
    // derived afresh from the weight exponential rather than adapted from what was
    // here. Nothing needs one today.
    //
    // The ceiling is still real and still has to be respected by hand:
    //
    //     (AmountLarge*ScaleLarge + AmountSmall*ScaleSmall) * G  <=  1
    //
    // where G is the largest |dTex/dUV| the filtered asset reaches -- one value now,
    // both octaves reading the same texture -- and each Amount is that octave's
    // Range.y, the worst case a region can resolve to. Past one the displacement
    // folds, neighbouring samples cross over, and the web tears rather than bends.
    // Neither octave is divergence-free any more, so there is no headroom above one
    // the way curl noise had -- see the domain-warp section above -- so this ceiling
    // is close to the real tear point rather than a conservative bound under it.
    // Widening WarpAmountLargeRange eats further into that margin on the inflated
    // end, since it is now possible for the large octave to exceed its old fixed
    // amount where it never could before.
    //
    // Practical version, unchanged: fix the scales, raise one range's max until the
    // web visibly tears, back off about thirty percent.
    P.bWarpEnable = ((InWarpAmountLargeRange.x != 0.0f) || (InWarpAmountLargeRange.y != 0.0f)
                  || (InWarpAmountSmallRange.x != 0.0f) || (InWarpAmountSmallRange.y != 0.0f))
                  ? 1.0f : 0.0f;

    // ONE TEST PER FETCH, listing exactly that fetch's consumers. Any of them being
    // NON-DEGENERATE (min != max, so the channel actually changes something) is enough
    // to pay for that fetch; a range with min == max is a constant, since VarianceRemap
    // returns it regardless of what channel or bias feeds it.
    //
    // SPLITTING THESE IS MOST OF WHY THE SECOND FETCH IS CHEAP. A layer that only varies
    // its appearance never pays for the structure fetch, and one that only varies its
    // structure never pays for the appearance fetch, so the common cases cost what they
    // did before the split.
    //
    // EVERY RANGE MUST APPEAR IN ITS FETCH'S LIST. A range that resolves per sample but
    // is missing reverts to its 0.5 midpoint whenever every OTHER range in that list
    // happens to be degenerate -- a pin that works until someone flattens the rest of
    // the group and then silently stops. A range that moves between fields has to move
    // between these lists with it.
    //
    // ONLY .x AND .y ARE TESTED, which matters now that four ranges carry an unrelated
    // scalar in .w. A range whose min equals its max is a constant however its .w is
    // set, so changing a warp scale must not make the layer think its amplitude varies.
    //
    // THE LATTICE BLEND IS THE ONE NON-RANGE ENTRY. It reads A.x through CellSizeBias
    // with no min/max of its own -- the two extremes it interpolates are the lattices
    // themselves -- so its test is whether there is a second lattice at all.
    P.bRegionEnableA = ((InVoidSizeSpreadRange.x != InVoidSizeSpreadRange.y)
                     || (InFeatureWidthRange.x != InFeatureWidthRange.y)
                     || (InWarpAmountLargeRange.x != InWarpAmountLargeRange.y)
                     || (ratio > 1.5f))
                     ? 1.0f : 0.0f;

    P.bRegionEnableB = ((InWallDensityRange.x != InWallDensityRange.y)
                     || (InWallFalloffRange.x != InWallFalloffRange.y)
                     || (InFilamentDensityRange.x != InFilamentDensityRange.y)
                     || (InVoidFloorRange.x != InVoidFloorRange.y)
                     || (InWarpAmountSmallRange.x != InWarpAmountSmallRange.y))
                     ? 1.0f : 0.0f;

    // See UNIVERSE_MIN_STEP_FRACTION. min of the two ends because a range may be
    // authored in reverse, and max against zero because a degenerate or negative width
    // should hand the floor back to the authored pin rather than drive it negative.
    float minWidth = max(min(InFeatureWidthRange.x, InFeatureWidthRange.y), 0.0f);
    P.MinFeatureStep = UNIVERSE_MIN_STEP_FRACTION * minWidth * cellSmall;

    P.BoundsFadeStart = InBoundsFadeStart;
    P.BoundsFadeStartSq = InBoundsFadeStart * InBoundsFadeStart;

    // Division guard again: a fade starting exactly at 1 has zero span.
    P.InvBoundsFadeSpan = 1.0f / max(1.0f - InBoundsFadeStart, 1e-6f);

    return P;
}
